\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Stronger After Measures Taken to Boost Liquidity\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes   \par \uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2012\par 20:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real strengthened Thursday following the government's easing of capital controls, suggesting it wants to avoid further weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.0772 to the dollar, after closing Wednesday at BRL2.0978, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSect.\par \par \uc2 "We had a problem of dollar liquidity and the government acted to solve that," said Sidnei Nehme, executive director at Brazilian brokerage NGO. "The market is quickly reacting to that," he added.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended the period of loans to qualify as export financing to five years from one. Those loans are exempt from the 6% financial-transactions tax, or IOF. The government also cut, from two years to one year, the maximum time limit of loans to which the IOF will apply.\par \par \uc2 "The government couldn't hold a spot auction, after complaining [earlier this year] that there was a tsunami of dollars coming into Brazil. So, they decided to ease the restrictions," said Mr. Nehme, who believes the easing of capital controls was adequate.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curtail the real's weakness after it approached BRL2.13 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the recent measures, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to go back to the trading range it kept for months, between BRL2.05 and BRL2.10 to the dollar, Mr. Nehme said. Still, he added, 2013 could again be a challenging year for the real, as the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , combined with historic low interest rates, have made \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  less attractive to overseas investors.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut the benchmark interest rate Selic from 12.50% in August 2011 to an unprecedented low level of 7.25%. Many market players expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the Selic at the current rate until the end of 2013.\par \par \uc2 In the minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy meeting on Nov. 27-28, released Thursday, the bank reaffirmed its commitment to keeping the interest rate stable for a "sufficiently prolonged period" to ensure inflation declines toward its 4.5% target.\par \par \uc2 Still, with growth weaker than expected in the third quarter, the market is sensing the possibility of more interest rate cuts next year. According to \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  strategist Dirk Willer, the term premium, as measured by the gap between market and economists' expectations, has turned negative, indicating the market is once again expecting more rate cuts than economists.\par \par \uc2 --Paulo Winterstein contributed to this report.\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 06-12-12 1912GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121206e8c6000yv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
