\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Closes Stronger on  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2012\par 19:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 --Market receives clear signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will defend BRL2.10\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL2.1175 to the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeff Fick \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's real closed sharply stronger against the U.S. dollar Monday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  responded to an early session test from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  three times in the local market, driving the real back toward the ceiling of its recent trading range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.1175 to the dollar, stronger from Friday's close at BRL2.1240, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. The real had started the session trading weaker from Friday's close, hovering around the BRL2.13 mark. Shortly after the bank's second of two swap auctions, the real briefly strengthened to BRL2.0969.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  returned to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in force during Monday's session after sitting on the sidelines last week, when the real broke through the ceiling of its recent BRL2.00-to-BRL2.10 trading range. The bank allowed long- and short-position holders to engage in their month-end tug-of-war to set the Ptax, the average daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate used to settle futures contracts, without unnecessary interference, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the party ended Monday, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it's become perfectly clear that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is going to defend BRL2.10," said Bruno Lavieri, an economist who tracks the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for Sao Paulo consultants group Tendencias. While the largest part of the real's depreciation against the dollar was testing by traders, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also could have been responding to a higher-than-normal demand for the greenback given the amount of interest in the three auctions held, Mr. Lavieri said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank ended Monday's session by announcing that it would sell up to $5 billion in dollars into the spot market. The bank did not immediately reveal the volume of dollars sold. The dollars were to be sold at Monday's Ptax rate of 2.1121, with repurchase by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set for Jan. 4 and Feb. 4, the bank said in the announcement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spot auction followed two separate swap auctions Monday. The bank effectively injected more than $3 billion in dollar liquidity into Brazil's futures market. The bank sold $1.09 billion and $997 million in dollar-swap contracts maturing Jan. 2. While $3.14 billion in reverse-swaps mature Monday, the bank had anticipated $1.62 billion Nov. 23 with a sale of swaps contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Today [the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] is not going to roll over the reverse-swap contracts and are making a complementary offer of swaps," said Sidnei Nehme, director at Sao Paul trading house NGO. "That's going to cause the real to correct its position. I think [the real] is going to strengthen further toward the comfort zone below BRL2.10."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BRL2.00-to-BRL2.10 range is seen as comfortable by the government because the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is weak enough to support local manufacturers and exporters without affecting inflation, traders said. But there has been increased market chatter since early November that the sluggish performance of Brazil's industrial sector could have the government thinking about letting the real weaken further to give manufacturers a boost.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In that case, Monday's action could be a move to smooth the path for a weaker real in the first quarter of 2013, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Yes, the government could be opening the door for the real to weaken past the BRL2.10 ceiling. Not necessarily right now, but in the coming months," said Tendencias' Mr. Lavieri. Should inflation end 2012 at current rates around 5.5%, planned cuts in electricity rates and accounting maneuvers would give the government enough space to allow the real to trade at BRL2.30 per dollar without fear of missing its inflation target for year-end 2013, the economist said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jeff Fick at Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 03-12-12 1900GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121203e8c3000uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
