\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Sharply Stronger on Heavy  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2012\par 14:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:14 EST - \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stifling talk of a new range for real, slapping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back into recent trading range by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the local market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real now trades at BRL2.1052 vs USD, sharply stronger from opening quote of BRL2.1297. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sold $1.09 billion in swaps shortly after open, now plans to sell up to an additional $1 billion in swaps. Auctions are a clear signal to market that bank is maintaining BRL2.10 ceiling of recent BRL2.00-BRL2.10 trading range, traders say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sat out late November volatile trade to not affect settlement of futures. (Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:14 EST - While admitting Deckers' (DECK) sales and margins should "remain challenged" through 1H, Sterne Agee says they "are likely to improve materially" in the back half of 2013 on improved offerings from the footwear and outdoor-apparel maker. Near-term worries have been a ballast around shares of the UGGs maker the past year, tumbling by 2/3 since Halloween 2011. But they jumped 1/3 last month to $38.29 and Sterne Agee sets a $65 target as it upgrades DECK to buy. The investment bank for the past year has "been hypercritical" of management actions "that we felt put the UGG brand at risk," including raising prices without offering much new and boosting distribution. Sterne Agee thinks officials have begun moving "to revitalize the UGG brand for fall 2013." DECK is up 10% premarket at $42.15. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:11 EST - A Reuters headline that Spain submitted a formal request for an EU bailout of its banking sector gave the euro a pop higher to its strongest level since Oct 23. But it's important to be clear: The headline refers only to a banking-sector bailout, which has already been months in the making. It's not a request for a broader sovereign bailout -- something the market's been waiting for when it comes to Spain. "The ability of the market to instantly react positively anyway, though, speaks volumes about the market bias," notes Kit Juckes at SocGen. The euro was at $1.3062, from $1.2986 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. It popped as high as $1.3075. (matthew.walter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:00 EST - An added thing for the mix on whether CONN investors should focus on the retailer's F3Q earnings or November sales slowdown is fresh equity bound to hit the market. Not quite half of the company's nearly 33M shares outstanding are in the public float, limiting the amount of stock available and potentially helping fuel 2012's price surge. But with current major holders planning to sell 3.6M shares and CONN offering at least 1.4M more, that will boost the amount of freely tradable stock by 1/3. That generally causes at least some near-term pressure on a stock. For now, CONN is up 1.7% premarket at $28.76. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:51 EST - Many investors have come to think there's hope in \uc2 Research In Motion\uc2  (RIMM) amid the coming launch of BlackBerry 10--the stock nearly doubled after hitting the latest in a series of 9-year lows in late September. But Canaccord isn't among those who have changed their opinions. While boosting its price target to $10 from $8, the investment bank drops its rating to sell from hold--a stance it held since September 2010. Then, Canaccord's target was $50. Today, it says, "Our checks and analysis of the global competitive landscape suggest a very-low probability BlackBerry 10 sales can turn around RIM's long-term business trends," with high-revenue users still moving to "sticky iPhone and Android ecosystems in droves." RIMM falls 3.6% premarket to $11.18. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:41 EST - Delta's (DAL) small presence at Heathrow is a big hole in its transatlantic network, but it's already the largest US carrier to and from Europe by capacity and has been picking up slots here and there at the London hub. So why mess with a \uc2 Virgin Atlantic\uc2  that would bring a quirky culture, an aircraft order book ripe for trimming and an eastward-facing route network that ebbs and flows with the tide? DAL hasn't commented on reported talks it might acquire up to 49% of \uc2 Virgin Atlantic\uc2 , to go with its recent investments in Brazil and Mexico. The Atlanta-based carrier must really, really want to be King of New York if it's turning to owning part of \uc2 Virgin Atlantic\uc2  to help build on its expanding presence at JFK and LaGuardia. (doug.cameron@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:38 EST - The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auction shows the BRL2.10/dollar ceiling of the real's recent trading range is intact, says Sidnei Nehme of trading house NGO. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the real to trade freely at the end of November to not affect settlement of month-end futures contracts. But now, it appears policymakers intend to bring the real back into line with prior trading. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is going to undercut the exchange rate" to bring the dollar to BRL2.05-2.10, the upper half of the BRL2-2.10 range it's been in since July, says Nehme. It's currently at BRL2.1120, versus BRL2.13 Friday. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - The numbers have been getting greener for US stock-index futures as the morning rolls on and the first trading day of December is here. Investor sentiment remains upbeat, as seen by the euro pushing toward $1.3050. That in general has helped given equities a lift. But with macro headlines still a market overhang, there's no reason to get overly optimistic about the bulls' latest push. S&P 500 futures are up 5 points as gold and Nymex oil futures are slightly higher. Treasurys are down slightly, with the 10-year yield at 1.63%. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:25 EST - It appears the "retirement" of \uc2 Pitney Bowes\uc2  (PBI) Chairman and CEO \uc2 Murray Martin\uc2  is less that and more of a "please go" as shares of the mail- and document-services company have continued to crumble this year as PBI has been trying to figure out its niche amid falling mail usage. It's selling its international mail business amid a belt-tightening effort as PBI looks for more enterprise-related business. The company's board is pinning its hopes on 27-year \uc2 IBM\uc2  veteran \uc2 Marc Lautenbach\uc2 , who has helped oversee Big Blue's business in the Americas since July 2005. New Chairman \uc2 Michael Roth\uc2  says Lautenbach "developed a reputation for delivering consistent results while assisting companies to reach their full potential." Martin because CEO in 2007; the stock has tumbled more than 3/4 since. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:10 EST - After disclosing another sharp gain in same-store-sales last month, CONN today unveils a sizable beat to F3Q EPS estimates and another boost to the year's profit expectations. The turnaround at the Texas-focused seller of capital goods like appliances and furniture has been well-documented at this point, and shares have more than doubled in 2012 to 5-year highs. However, there could be some profit-taking today as SSS were merely decent last month, rising 6% after 10% growth in November 2011. The latest figure is a slower pace than what the company has been recording, but comps are getting tougher. Still, with the run shares have been on this year, it's hard to argue CONN hasn't been priced toward perfection lately. Shares are inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:40 EST - Investor sentiment has been notably blah about Intel (INTC) amid the PC industry's struggles this year, with shares down 1/3 since hitting a 8-year high in May. Sterne Agee adds to the concern this morning, saying its channel checks show analysts may be too upbeat about 1Q PC production. It sees output being close to seasonal averages for the start of a year--falling 7-10% from 4Q--not dropping 3% as the investment bank says consensus is now around for next quarter. Coupled with INTC's "very high" inventories, Sterne Agee cuts its estimates on the chip maker, starting with this quarter, and trims $1 off its price target, to $18. INTC is up 0.7% in light premarket trading, after dropping 9.5% in November, to $19.70. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:20 EST - US stock-index futures are a bit in the green as a seasonally strong month for equities begins. Those gains have started early this year as a dour 1H of November was followed by a strong rebound the past 2 weeks. Besides Santa Claus rallies and the macro front, there's probably not much to move equities at this point up or down from the corporate front with earnings season over--unless a spate of fresh 4Q forecasts roll in before year-end. Dow-industrial futures rise 25 points and the S&P 500 is up 2.5. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bradley Davis\f28 \uc2 \f2 , 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-03-12 0814ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121203e8c30003w\par }\p