\par\par \b \uc2 3Q Notwithstanding, Brazil's Economy Still Poised to Rebound - Goldman's O'Neill\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2012\par 22:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil will likely grow 1.7% in 2012 - O'Neill\par \par \uc2 --Brazil can grow 4% or more in 2013 - O'Neill\par \par \uc2 --Brazil's real still overvalued - O'Neill\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Luciana Magalhaes \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO--Despite Brazil's disappointing economic performance in the third quarter, Latin America's biggest economy should still rebound with growth of 4% or more in 2013, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jim O'Neill\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the chairman of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Asset Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was just one quarter," said Mr. O'Neill, who coined the famous acronym "BRIC," grouping the world's heavyweight emerging markets Brazil, Russia, India and China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. O'Neill's growth forecast for next year is above market consensus. He bases it on the hefty monetary and fiscal stimulus that Brazil's government has pumped into the economy this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the real, is still overvalued, he said. Even though the real has lost 11% of its value against the dollar so far this year, Mr. O'Neill added that it could weaken further. That, in turn, would boost the competitiveness of the country's exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's gross domestic product expanded 0.9% in the third quarter compared with the third quarter of 2011, according to official data out Friday, far less than the expected 2.3%. On a quarterly basis, total economic output in July-through-September rose just 0.6%, making for an annualized 2.4% rate of growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an email, Mr. O'Neill said the weakness was very specific to the financial sector, suggesting it was temporary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guido Mantega\f28 \uc2 \f2  said much the same thing following the data release. The banking industry was hurt by a combination of lower lending and lower interest rates, Mr. Mantega told reporters. But lower interest rates should start to feed into higher lending, which will help banks recover, Mr. Mantega added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. O'Neill estimates the Brazilian economy will expand around 1.7% in 2012, and 4% or more next year. His forecasts are above market consensus of 1.5% growth this year and 3.94% in 2013. But the current consensus was polled before the data came out, so it could soon be revised even lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. O'Neill suggested the market isn't taking into account just how much the monetary easing and some fiscal initiatives will likely catapult growth next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has cut the Selic base rate continuously since August 2011, when it stood at 12.50%, to a historic low of 7.25%. It has also cut taxes on consumer goods, aiming to fuel economic activity. "These are leading indicators of future growth."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the real \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Mr. O'Neill said that, despite its slide this year, an even lower exchange rate would be to the country's benefit. "Something closer to BRL2.30 or BRL2.35 to the dollar would be good for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and fiscal measures taken by the government to protect local exporters have kept the real trading in a limited range between BRL2.00 to around BRL2.10 in recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It broke through the weaker end of that range Friday in the wake of the data, exiting active trading at BRL2.1240 to the dollar, losing significant ground from Thursday's close at BRL2.0955.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. O'Neill said if interest rates remain at their current level or go even lower in face of the weak growth, the "real will likely weaken further."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday left its key rate unchanged at 7.25%, and suggested in its statement that it would remain at that level for an extended period of time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest GDP data, Mr. O'Neill said, doesn't change his views on Brazil or reduce the country's position in the global economy. "It is one of top 10 economies by size in the world, probably still sixth or seventh," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Asset Management has $716.1 billion in assets under management, according to the company's website.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 11-30-12 1642ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020121130e8bu0006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
