\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Disappointing Start to US Consumers' 4Q\b0\par\par\uc2 1,329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2012\par 14:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:35 EST - Today's income and spending report disappoints with flat income and a 0.2% drop in spending. Commerce says it can't determine precisely the impact from Sandy, although there was some effect. Today's numbers -- including a 0.3% drop in real consumer spending -- mean household sector started 4Q very weakly, at same time that businesses are holding back. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:28 EST - The safe-haven market got some support from month-end buying. Fund managers need to buy bonds to match the adjustment of their benchmark indices. Uncertainty over the US fiscal cliff also supported the market, though intra-day price swings have been notable in recent sessions on headlines on the progress -- or lack of progress -- on a fiscal cliff deal. Personal spending data are due at 8:30 a.m. ET. The 10-year yield continues to trade between 1.55%-1.85%. The 10-year note is 5/32 higher to yield 1.603%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:19 EST - USD/CAD stretches upward, as the market awaits 3Q Canadian GDP. Consensus is for a 0.8% annualized print, softer than the 1% the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's forecast in October. "No one within the forecast consensus is looking for miracles," says Scotiabank's Derek Holt. Monthly GDP releases point to a 0.6%-0.7% pace of quarterly growth, (assuming the expected 0.1% expansion for September is accurate). Holt warns even the tepid 0.1% September growth might be overshooting, because September retail sales volumes were flat, housing starts fell, wholesale trade volumes were weak, and there was overall weakness in the housing sector that might carry over into services output. US dollar is at C$0.9939, from C$0.9923 late Thursday. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:18 EST - The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market probably has other things on its mind than today's US economic data, says Kit Juckes of \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . For one thing, indicators like the Chicago PMI are likely to have been blurred by supply disruptions resulting from Hurricane Sandy. Juckes contends now that the next tranche of financial aid for Greece is seen as more or less a done deal, the market is focused on what he calls "the dreadful seesaw" of fiscal-cliff news, with bouts of buying and selling being dictated chiefly by the headlines coming out of Washington. The dollar is up strongly against the yen at Y82.63 but is down a bit versus the euro at around $1.2990. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:01 EST - While \uc2 La-Z-Boy\uc2  (LZB) logged a F2Q miss and saw its stock slide 9.7% Thursday, \uc2 Raymond James\uc2  has hiked its price target on the stock to $18 from $16.50, saying, "There is a lot to be encouraged about...The company is clearly gaining market share, its long-underperforming retail division is on the verge of profitability and the company's financial condition is sound. Housing, which has been a significant headwind for years, has begun to slowly heal." Then there's valuation, which at 10 times forward P/E is in line with the 10-year median despite \uc2 Raymond James\uc2  thinking sales and margins could rise significantly starting next year. LZB closed Thursday at $14.60. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:52 EST - Brazil's stimulus measures have "simply reignited the old consumption-led growth model" rather than spurring productive investment, leading to disappointing third-quarter growth, Capital Economics says. While there are some signs that a weak real and import restrictions are helping out Brazilian manufacturing, growth prospects for coming quarters are "lackluster" and leave open the door for additional interest-rate cuts next year to support growth, economist Neil Shearing writes. (paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:45 EST - While a disappointing 3Q GDP number is partly behind the weakening of the Brazilian real against the dollar, the end-of-the-month battle among market players to influence the Ptax rate--used to settle futures contracts--is having more impact, says Luiz Carlos Baldan at brokerage Fourtrade. He notes there was even more \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility Thursday than so far today. Baldan doesn't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Friday as it accepts the volatility associated with Ptax. "If they do something, it's going to be in beginning of December." (luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:41 EST - A nice F3Q EPS beat for specialty-apparel retailer Genesco (GCO) as the company continued to see strong same-store-sales growth at most of its chains. That includes 8% at Journeys and 6% at \uc2 Johnston & Murphy\uc2 . But Lids had a 5% drop and GCO also saw broad weakness this month; companywide SSS are down 4% after rising 12% in November 2011. But only a bit of that drop--a percentage point or 2--is attributable to Sandy, says GCO. Numerous retailers yesterday blamed their weak sales this month on the hurricane. Meanwhile, last weekend's US comps only rose by a low-single-digit percentage. But amid the 3Q profit beat, GCO does raise its FY EPS target. Shares are up 0.7% premarket at $61. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:34 EST - Brazil's economy will grow much less than previously expected in 2012 after a disappointing third quarter, says Rafael Bacciotti of consultants group Tendencias. Economists had expected growth of 1.5% in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey earlier this week, but Brazil's now looking at an expansion of 1% in 2012, economist says. Third-quarter growth of 0.6% "was a big surprise," Bacciotti says. Tendencias still working through data, but already expects to lower 2013 outlook to closer to 3% from previous forecast of 3.6%. Growth hurt by gloomy global outlook and \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies that undermine business investment, economist says. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - As risk assets in general are poised to end November on a positive note, it goes without saying the uncertainly which still lies out there. And those are just the known issues--like Greece and the fiscal cliff. Nevermind things that are surprises, like the egg Brazil laid this morning with its 3Q GDP. That further highlights how former emerging-market growth engines are stalling as well. On tap today for US economic data is October personal income and spending at 8:30 and the November Chicago PMI at 9:45. S&P 500 futures rise 2 as oil, and gold are up slightly along with the euro, which is sitting right around $1.30. Treasurys are flat, with the 10-year yield at 1.62%. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:16 EST - Chilean peso strengthens slightly against the dollar Friday tracking higher copper prices after signs of growth in data out of the U.S. and China Thursday. Peso often takes cues from copper prices as the metal is Chile's main export. As Chile's unemployment data came in within expectations, traders are waiting for U.S. economic data and tracking equities. Dollar recently trading at CLP478.70 versus Thursday's close of CLP479, according to Valor Futuro newswire. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:13 EST - Brazilian rates are down across the board as traders bet there may be more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Wednesday's rate-cutting pause. One trader notes GDP estimates will fall and the real will drop further, stoking the potential of further reduction to the Selic, already at a record low 7.25%. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bradley Davis\f28 \uc2 \f2 , 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-30-12 0835ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121130e8bu00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
