\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Argentine debt rallies; Brazil real slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2012\par 18:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Optimism on U.S. budget deal aids most Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Dollar-long players seek weaker month-end Brazil real rate\par \par \uc2 * Real weakens 0.33 pct, Mexican peso gains 0.24 pct\par \par \uc2 (Updates with Argentine bond rally, previous SAO PAULO)\par \par \uc2 By Jorge Otaola\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 29 (Reuters) - Argentine bonds rallied on Thursday after the country won a reprieve from a U.S. appeals court that eased investors' fears of a new default as early as next month.\par \par \uc2 Argentine debt prices had tumbled since a U.S. court ruled last week that the country must pay $1.33 billion by Dec. 15 to "holdout" investors who rejected a previous restructuring of its defaulted debt.\par \par \uc2 The South American nation had rejected paying the investors, who had filed the lawsuit, raising the risk of a technical default on about $24 billion worth of debt since Argentina was going to be barred from trying to only pay investors who had taken part in two debt exchanges in 2005 and 2010.\par \par \uc2 But an appeals court on Wednesday gave Argentina more time to fight last week's ruling by U.S. District Judge Thomas Griesa.\par \par \uc2 "For a few days at least, happiness is back," said Sabrina Corujo and analyst at consultancy Portfolio Personal.\par \par \uc2 After slumping nearly 30 percent since late October, the price on Argentina's June 2017 global bond jumped almost 14 percent on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the Brazilian real extended its losses for a second day on Thursday, under pressure by dollar-heavy investors who wanted to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday set a monthly exchange reference rate.\par \par \uc2 The real bid down 0.33 percent to 2.0963 per dollar, recovering from a drop of around 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The only thing that justifies this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move is the fight over the Ptax," said Mario Battistel, manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading desk at Fair brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The Ptax is a benchmark exchange rate for a broad range of contracts, including foreign loans, trade, and derivatives. Dollar-laden speculators may have tried to keep the real weak through the Ptax fix, traders said. A higher Ptax means it costs more reais to buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 The real pared steeper losses as investors feared that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to slide.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday, as the real traded at a 3-1/2 year low of around 2.11 per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an auction of traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, derivative contracts that emulate the sale of dollars in the futures market.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers have used both verbal and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the real largely within a range of 2 per dollar to 2.05 since July.\par \par \uc2 But other analysts said authorities may be leaning toward allowing for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exporters, and they may allow the real to drift lower, as long as the drop does not happen with too much velocity.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained along with emerging market stocks. Boosting investors' appetite for risk was optimism that U.S. leaders could reach a deal to avert steep spending cuts and tax hikes that could plunge the world's largest economy in recession next year.\par \par \uc2 "In the United States, they say there are growing chances of an agreement to avoid the fiscal cliff and that is bring optimism to the market," said Gloria Soto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at FXCM Chile.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso firmed 0.24 percent and the Chilean peso gained 0.33 percent.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2140 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        daily  year-to-d\par                                    %       te %\par                       Latest  change     change\par Brazil real             2.0963   -0.33      -10.9\par                                       \par Mexico peso            12.9381    0.24        8.0\par                                       \par Argentina peso*         6.4200    0.78      -26.3\par                                       \par Chile peso            479.0000    0.33        8.4\par                                       \par Colombia peso       1,815.1000    0.45        6.8\par                                       \par Peru sol                2.5760    0.35        4.7\par                                       \par * Argentine peso's rate                          \par between brokerages                      \par                                                \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Moise Avila in Santiago and Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City; Editing by Leslie Gevirtz)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | E | U | M | RBN | NAT | D | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121202e8bt0008w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich2