\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real jumps as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  eclipses Mantega\b0\par\par\uc2 820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2012\par 17:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, other Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; updates prices)\par \par \uc2 * Real weakens to 2.11/dollar as Mantega says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate 'reasonable'\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  calls swap auction, props real back to 2.08/dlr\par \par \uc2 * Hopes of Greece deal support Mexican peso, which gains 0.6 pct\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte and Natalia Cacioli\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Nov 23 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real\par \par \uc2 on Friday posted its largest single-day gain in nearly three months after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted to halt losses that had taken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the limits of an informal trading band.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso also posted gains, buoyed by hopes that Greece's lenders were nearing an agreement to release funds to Athens, but the Chilean peso dropped after hitting a one-month high in the previous session.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market came as the real quickly slid on comments by Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2 , who told a crowd of business leaders in Sao Paulo that the exchange rate is at a "reasonable though not totally satisfactory level" to support industry.\par \par \uc2 The apparent tug of war between Mantega and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left investors wondering whether policymakers still uphold an informal trading band of 2.0-2.1 per dollar where the real has been stuck since early July.\par \par \uc2 "The government is worried about industry, as we can tell by comments from several officials, but then comes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  worried about inflation and you have this tug of war," said a trader with a Brazilian bank.\par \par \uc2 "We can't say, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is defending the level of 2.1 per dollar. It only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  today because the real was weakening too sharply," he added.\par \par \uc2 The real gained 0.8 percent to 2.0812 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about half of the 62,800 traditional swaps it offered in an auction.\par \par \uc2 Those contracts, which emulate the sale of dollars in the futures market, were apparently auctioned in an attempt to cancel some reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps -- which mimic the purchase of dollars -- expiring in the beginning of December.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the swap auction almost at the same time as Mantega said the exchange rate isn't totally satisfactory," said Alvaro Bandeira, chief economist with Orama in Rio de Janeiro. "The market is chaotic but so is the government."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shortly after the real slid more than 0.8 percent to an intraday low of 2.1168 per dollar -- its weakest in 3-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 Hinting that some type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was possible, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  had already said on Thursday that the bank was ready to provide liquidity to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at year-end, when dollars are traditionally more scarce in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "Today's (early) losses in the real were out of control and did not reflect the fundamentals," said Jankiel Santos, chief economist at BES Investimento in Sao Paulo. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to avoid market excess."\par \par \uc2 NEW TRADING RANGE?\par \par \uc2 The real had been trading weaker than 2.1 per dollar since the beginning of the session as small dollar outflows weighed on a market with thin trading volumes after the Thanksgiving holiday in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The 2.0-2.1 reais per dollar range, informally imposed by policymakers through a series of market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past several months, was considered at the same time favorable to Brazilian exporters and not too bad for inflation.\par \par \uc2 But signs that the government would favor a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the economy started to emerge early this week, when President Dilma Rousseff said in an interview that the government is "looking for an exchange rate that is not this one, with a devalued dollar and an over-valued real."\par \par \uc2 A weaker real could help Brazil's manufacturers, which have struggled with an over-valued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high input costs. The real has lost 10 percent this year, but remains more than 20 percent stronger than it was at the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 Mantega, who coined the expression "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" in 2010 to complain about the strength of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , indicated that the real is approaching the levels desired by the government, although he made clear the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has some room to weaken.\par \par \uc2 Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2045 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 