\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Weaker  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Might Not Spark  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2012\par 13:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:28 EST - The weakening of the Brazilian real past the level of BRL2.1 to the dollar, thought by many in the market to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unofficial ceiling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , might not provoke a reaction from the bank after all, said Darwin Dib, economist at CM Capital markets in Sao Paulo. The weaker real is only worrisome because of its potential inflationary effect, and it would take a prolonged move past the BRL2.1 level for that effect to manifest itself. A short-term move past the level is a concern for trading desks, but not for the government, Dib said. (jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:21 EST - Chilean peso is nearly unchanged against the dollar in a quiet session after the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday. Copper prices are also nearly flat in thinned trade. The peso often takes its cues from copper prices as the metal is the Andean country's main export and accounts for 15% of GDP. Dollar recently trading at CLP476.60 versus Thursday's close of CLP476.50, according to Valor Futuro newswire. (graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:18 EST - \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may indeed be opening up more room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to fluctuate, after several months of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -imposed stagnation, a trader acknowledges, agreeing with Ex-Fin Min Antonio Delfim Neto. In a Dow Jones Newswires/Wall Street Journal interview yesterday, Delfim Neto said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to avoid becoming a prisoner of a tight trading range. "Although I am not a big fan of Delfim, it makes sense," says the trader. "Too much time without volatility is an invitation to players mess up with leverage," says the trader. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-23-12 0728ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121123e8bn0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
