\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-ANALYSIS-Brazil likely to let real slip, but not much\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2012\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Rousseff likely to let real weaken beyond 2.10/dlr\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent volatility\par \par \uc2 * Inflation fears mean 2.12 or 2.15/dlr could be new floor\par \par \uc2 (Updates with Mantega, market movement, analyst comment)\par \par \uc2 By Brian Winter\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff is likely to loosen the informal trading band for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and let the real gradually weaken beyond 2.10 per dollar, but such an adjustment will be minor because of concerns over inflation.\par \par \uc2 The real was about 0.7 percent weaker in late trading on Wednesday at 2.095 per dollar, following rising aversion to riskier assets in global markets.\par \par \uc2 Traders were eager to see whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, as it has done in recent months when the real threatened to break out of its de facto trading band of 2 to 2.10 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff's recent statements on the real, plus comments from other senior officials, suggest the government will tolerate a slightly weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming weeks if the market continues to push it in that direction.\par \par \uc2 A weaker real could help Brazil's manufacturers, which have struggled with an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high input costs. The real is 12 percent weaker this year, but is still 19 percent stronger than it was in 2008.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent an excessively quick depreciation that could destabilize the broader economy. It will likely tolerate only a small shift in the trading band, to 2.12 or 2.15 at most in the near term, because of concerns a weaker real could complicate the bank's inflation target in 2013 as economic activity picks up.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  told reporters on Wednesday that the real was "fluctuating normally" and that the government was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as little as possible in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - comments that indicated a lack of concern as the real crept closer to 2.10.\par \par \uc2 In some of her most explicit comments about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in recent months, Rousseff told Valor Economico newspaper this week that "we're looking for an exchange rate that is not this one, with a devalued dollar and an overvalued real."\par \par \uc2 Those comments helped push the real lower on Wednesday. The session was also unusually volatile because many traders were on holiday on Monday and Tuesday, and because of the coming Thanksgiving holiday in the United States.\par \par \uc2 WORRIES OVER INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Influential Valor columnist Cristiano Romero wrote on Wednesday that the government may be seeking a significantly weaker real in the medium term, at 2.20 to 2.25 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 However, some in the market thought that view was a misinterpretation of Rousseff's comments.\par \par \uc2 "We do not agree with this reading and believe that the ceiling is closer to 2.10 than it is to 2.15," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a note to clients on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "With economic activity gaining momentum, another round of (real) depreciation could have a larger effect on inflation, which is an event we do not believe the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is looking for," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets expect that consumer inflation could hit 5.39 percent next year - above the mid-point of the government's inflation target of 4.5 percent, with a tolerance band of 2 percentage points in each direction.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts expressed concern that the apparent policy shift could signal a softer government stance on inflation - something officials rigorously deny.\par \par \uc2 "It seems to me that there is a coordinated policy effort ... which signals that they can live with a weaker real," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, an analyst with RBS Securities.\par \par \uc2 "But I don't see them having that much conviction on the inflation side. They seen to be ready to (let the real weaken) and that raises the question if they are also letting inflation go. This is negative for Brazil."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Alonso Soto. Editing by Theodore d'Afflisio and Andre Grenon)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS, UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | ABX | BNX | FUN | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121124e8bl00074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
