\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Weaker on Global Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2012\par 19:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real exits active trading at BRL2.0747/Dlr vs BRL2.0659 Wednesday\par \par \uc2 --Investors take "protective" dollar positions in spot, futures markets\par \par \uc2 --Investors worried about U.S., Europe fiscal problems, Mideast violence\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real weakened against the U.S. dollar Friday as investors took "protective" dollar positions ahead of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.0747 to the dollar, weaker against the Wednesday close of BRL2.0659, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. Thursday was a national holiday in Brazil, with all financial markets closed.\par \par \uc2 "The global situation has turned even more delicate than usual," said Luiz Carlos Baldan, director of the Fourtrade brokerage in Sao Paulo. "Investors here in Brazil want to be holding dollars, not reais, ahead of the weekend. There could be news on any of several fronts over the weekend--Europe, the Middle East or the United States."\par \par \uc2 Europe is struggling with continued worries over the Greek debt crisis while the U.S. is riveted on political negotiations over crucial tax and spending issues that must be resolved by the end of the year. The Middle East this week erupted in violence between Palestinians in the Gaza Strip and Israel, sending global oil prices higher.\par \par \uc2 The real was also weaker in futures trading Friday, a key factor in the Brazilian market because of high trading volumes in the futures market. On the BM&FBovespa exchange, in late-afternoon trading the December \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures contract saw the real weaken to BRL2.083 to the dollar while the January contract weakened to BRL2.098 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real briefly weakened to BRL2.08 to the dollar in the spot market Friday, its weakest level since June.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, many traders had offered the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at the BRL2.08 to-the-dollar level in an effort to prevent a disorderly depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weaker real could bring inflationary pressures, especially as imports of consumer goods and even some perishables tend to rise ahead of the Christmas season.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sidelined Friday. Traders noted that the real quickly backed away from the BRL2.08 to-the-dollar level as exporters sold dollars to the market.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baldan added, "The market was a little more volatile than usual Friday because of thin volume. One or two transactions can send the exchange rate flying in one direction or the other."\par \par \uc2 Volume was thin after Thursday's national holiday, Brazil's Day of the Republic.\par \par \uc2 Volume is likely to be thin again Monday ahead of a Tuesday holiday in Brazil, Black Consciousness Day.\par \par \uc2 "I think we'll see the real gain a little strength when trading resumes at normal volumes next Wednesday," said Mr. Baldan. "I don't think we're heading to BRL2.10 to the dollar."\par \par \uc2 -Write to \uc2 Tom Murphy\uc2  at tom.murphy@dowjones.com [ 16-11-12 1823GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121116e8bg000tv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
