\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Opens Stronger on China Data, Shrugs Off Greece Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2012\par 12:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Brazil's real opens stronger vs. U.S. dollar as China manufacturing data improve\par \par \uc2 --Local markets are cautious ahead of Friday's U.S. jobs data, next week's election\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real opens at BRL2.0294, stronger from Wednesday's close at BRL2.0301 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeff Fick \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--The Brazilian real traded stronger against the U.S. dollar at the open Thursday as data from key trading partner China that showed improvement in the Asian country's manufacturing sector offset ongoing concerns about Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real shrugged off a slide by the euro, a key barometer for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , amid renewed concerns about Greece's ability to implement austerity measures needed for its rescue package. The real opened trading at BRL2.0294 to the U.S. dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was stronger than Wednesday's close at BRL2.0301.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  received a boost after manufacturing data in China showed signs of improvement in October, a sign that the country's industrial sector could be rebounding. China's official Purchasing Managers Index rose to 50.2 in October from 49.8 in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is Brazil's largest trading partner, importing huge amounts of commodities such as iron ore and soy from Latin America's largest country. A dramatic slowdown in the Asian country would have a big impact on local commodities exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for manufacturing was much gloomier in Brazil, where industrial production fell a larger-than-expected 1.0% in September from August. Economists had expected a 0.5% decline. Brazil's manufacturing sector has struggled for more than a year, despite massive reductions in local interest rates and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets that has caused the real to weaken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local markets will likely take further direction from overseas data releases, although players are expected to remain cautious ahead of a Brazilian holiday that will close financial markets on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data out of the U.S. should give a better picture of the health of the world's largest economy heading into next week's presidential election. Employment figures and the Institute for Supply Management's activity index will be released later Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While changes to the methodology of the employment report make it less important, Kit Juckes of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that "a bounce in the ISM index would support a view that the U.S. economy, like China's, is turning a corner." Mr. Juckes said that the risk of the U.S. sliding back into recession appears to be receding, "which keeps the market focus, for now, firmly on hunting for yield."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The hunt for yield, however, is unlikely to hit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continuing to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and tough government rhetoric keeping the real locked in its recent trading range. The real continues to trade between BRL2.00 and BRL2.05 to the U.S. dollar, a level that the government considers comfortable, traders said. According to traders, the real is seen as weak enough to support local manufacturers and exporters without affecting inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent upswing in capital outflows could test the government's willingness to let the real depreciate further against the dollar, risking inflationary pressures. Brazil registered net capital outflows of $748 million in the first four weeks of October, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday. While a rise in imports was behind the outflows, there are concerns that negative investment flows could emerge through the end of the year if foreign investors bail out of local stocks or multinational companies start to repatriate capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jeff Fick at Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 01, 2012 07:48 ET (11:48 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121101e8b1003et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
