\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  seesaw, end flat with Wall St\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2012\par 21:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Wall St little changed after storm-related closure\par \par \uc2 * Brazil trade minister says gov't wants real at 2/dlr\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso dips 0.1 pct, Brazil real flat\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Oct 31 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso edged up and down on Wednesday, closely tracking U.S. stock markets, while the Chilean peso posted losses as investors took defensive positions before a long holiday.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed practically flat, repeating the same performance it had during most of October, when repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  drastically reduced the volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Investors' appetite for risk was greatly influenced by the performance of Wall Street, which resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  after storm Sandy shut down trading for two sessions. Key U.S. stock indexes lacked clear direction, however, as investors assessed the economic impact of the storm.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the uncertainty, many investors were rebalancing their portfolios in the last day of the month.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso seesawed between positive and negative territories during most of the session to dip 0.1 percent later on the day.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso lost 0.35 percent to 481.00 per greenback, however, ahead of a two-day holiday that will keep local markets closed for the rest of the week.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in the peso is simply due to increased demand for dollars to cover positions. That is a precautionary measure by investors worried about what might happen (in external markets) during the holiday," said a trader in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real ended at 2.0397 per dollar, practically flat for the day and around 0.1 percent weaker for the month.\par \par \uc2 Reinforcing expectations that the real will remain at the current level for a while, Brazil's Trade Minister Fernando Pimentel said on Wednesday that the government is focused on keeping the exchange rate at 2 reais per dollar to protect the local industry.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2000 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  Year-to\par                                      pct     date\par                          Latest   change     pct \par                                            change\par Brazil real                2.0307    -0.06    -7.99\par                                           \par Mexico peso               13.0999    -0.10     6.64\par                                           \par Argentina peso*            6.3200     0.63   -25.16\par                                           \par Chile peso               481.0000    -0.35     7.96\par                                           \par Colombia peso          1,831.6500    -0.05     5.83\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5960    -0.08     3.89\par                                           \par * Argentine peso's rate between                    \par brokerages                                  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Froilan Romero in Santiago; editing by Andrew Hay)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121104e8av00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscale