\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Stronger as Global Market Steadies\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2012\par 14:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real rebounded moderately against the dollar at the start of trading Wednesday, as international markets steadied after the previous session's slide.\par \par \uc2 Around 8:15 a.m. EDT, the real was quoted in Sao Paulo trading at BRL2.2042 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, stronger than Tuesday's close of BRL2.0255.\par \par \uc2 Weak corporate third-quarter earnings have soured investor sentiment in recent sessions, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average posting its biggest one-day drop in four months Tuesday. Stock-index futures pointed to a positive open in the U.S. and Brazil Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The real has only reacted slightly to such movements, however, with the international selloff and a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday sending the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  about 0.1% weaker against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the equivalent of $1.648 billion in dollar-linked contracts at the start of the previous session in a so-called reverse-swap auction.\par \par \uc2 Financial-transactions taxes imposed by Brazil's government over the past couple of years, and frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures market, have led the real to practically regulate itself within a narrow trading range of BRL2 to BRL2.05 in recent months. Many market participants believe the band strikes the appropriate balance--in the government's eyes--of allowing local industry and exporters to be competitive without stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is a dirty float, same as everybody," Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  said in an interview published Wednesday in the Brazilian financial daily Valor Economico.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega and his counterparts have long defended their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market by arguing that loose monetary policy has artificially weakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developed countries, particularly the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "For us, the ideal is a floating exchange, with no manipulation," the official said. "However, if everybody starts to manipulate [exchange rates], we will do it too. We can't see others taking our market and collapsing our industry."\par \par \uc2 Write to Paul Kiernan at paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 24-10-12 1235GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121024e8ao000ty\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
