\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , European Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 14:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real opens weaker from Monday at BRL2.0272/dlr\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds reverse-swap auction, weakening real\par \par \uc2 --Euro, commodities weaken on renewed worries about Spain\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tom Murphy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real opened weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday following disclosure of a reverse-swap auction by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real opened at BRL2.0272 to the dollar, weaker against the Monday close of BRL2.0235, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the start of trading, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  called a reverse-swap auction, offering to exchange contracts worth up to the equivalent of $3 billion. In the auction, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actually exchange the equivalent of $1.648 billion in contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reverse-swap auctions allow investors to exchange contracts linked to the U.S. dollar for paper indexed to domestic interest rates. Reverse-swap auctions tend to support the U.S. dollar against the Brazilian real because they remove dollar-hedged contracts from the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reverse-swap auctions are part of a government campaign to avoid any significant appreciation of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar. Officials have said a strong real hurts exporters and manufacturers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent weeks, the real has at times signaled a strengthening against the dollar because of foreign-investment inflows. The most-recent inflows have been coming from a spate of overseas bonds issued by Brazilian companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're going to see this narrow band, just above BRL2.00 to the dollar, for quite some time," said Mario Battistel, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Sao Paulo's Fair brokerage. "It could continue this way well into 2013."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real was also weakening Tuesday because of renewed worries about the European debt crisis. A report by Spain's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that country was facing a deeper recession than previously expected. Spain's declining economy could cause it to miss crucial fiscal-performance targets, according to analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European worries led to a weakening of the euro against the U.S. dollar as well as declining commodities prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lower commodities prices hurt Brazilian exports and tend to weaken the real.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tom Murphy\f28 \uc2 \f2  at tom.murphy@dowjones.com. [ 23-10-12 1211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121023e8an000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
