\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Brazil current account gap wider on weaker trade surplus\b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 15:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil posts larger-than-expected $2.6 bln c/a deficit\par \par \uc2 * Foreign direct investment falls to $4.4 bln in Sept\par \par \uc2 * Cbank sees FDI rebounding to $6 bln in October\par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst comments and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates)\par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto and Tiago Pariz\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Oct 23 (Reuters) - Brazil's current account deficit widened in September from a year ago as a weakening global economy drags down exports from the South American nation, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Brazil had a current account deficit of $2.596 billion in September, stable from the gap posted in August, but above the $2.45 billion median forecast of analysts surveyed by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit, a broad measure of a country's foreign transactions including trade in goods and services but also profit remittances and interest payments, in September of last year was $2.234 billion.\par \par \uc2 "The current account remains well anchored: the erosion in the trade balance in the year to date has been more than offset by the decline in net remittances of profits and dividends," Alberto Ramos, chief Latin America economist for \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 The repatriation of profits abroad was only $1.1 billion in September, down from $1.9 billion in September of last year and $2.5 billion in August. The fall in remittances reflects the drop in profits by foreign companies operating in Brazil as both the local and global economy struggle to recover.\par \par \uc2 A strong flow of foreign investment in Brazil despite the weak global economy has so far helped cover the country's current account gap this year.\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment in Latin America's largest economy was $4.393 billion in September, down from $5.03 billion in August and more than the expected $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 The bank's head of economic research Tulio Maciel said he expects FDI to rise again to $6 billion in October based on preliminary data.\par \par \uc2 President Dilma Rousseff's government efforts to open up public infrastructure concessions to private investors and major sporting events like the 2014 Soccer World Cup are expected to attract investors in coming years.\par \par \uc2 Signs of a recovery in local economic activity is also expected to bring in more foreign capital.\par \par \uc2 PORTFOLIO INVESTMENT SEEN UP\par \par \uc2 Portfolio inflows, which along with FDI is part of the capital account, fell sharply to $983 million from $2.141 billion in August mostly due the sale of local shares. Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index has fallen about 16 percent since hitting the year's high in early March.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' Ramos said the weak capital account numbers mirror the shift in foreign investors' sentiment as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures by the local government.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slapped taxes on some capital inflows as a way to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, from gaining too much.\par \par \uc2 In the first nine months of the year, foreign investment in local shares tumbled to $2.89 billion from $5.95 billion in the same period last year. Foreign investment in local debt has remained mostly stable in that same period.\par \par \uc2 Still, the bank's Maciel said preliminary data shows a strong recovery in foreign investment in local shares. Foreign investment in shares was up to $1.2 billion as of Oct. 19.\par \par \uc2 Last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its 2012 current account deficit estimate to $53 billion from $56 billion.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the trade ministry reported that Brazil's trade surplus narrowed for the sixth straight month to $ 2.557 billion in September, as a slowing global economy undermined prices of the commodity powerhouse's key exports.\par \par \uc2 The current account deficit in the 12 months through September was equal to 2.15 percent of the country's gross domestic product, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, up from a previously reported 2.12 percent in August.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Luciana Otoni; Writing by Alonso Soto)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/CORRENTACCOUNT (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121023e8an000u9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
