\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Slightly Stronger After Rate Cut\b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2012\par 15:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --The real traded at BRL2.0349 from BRL2.0384 at Wednesday's close\par \par \uc2 --Real still range-bound as markets expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Spain ratings cut, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate cut not enough to break out of range\par \par \uc2 By Jeffrey T. Lewis\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--The Brazilian real opened slightly stronger against the dollar, keeping within the range of recent months amid expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep it from strengthening.\par \par \uc2 The real traded at BRL2.0349 early in the session, after closing at BRL2.0384 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The real has traded within a range of about BRL2.01 to BRL2.04 to the dollar in recent months because the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has directly entered the market to protect the level of BRL2.0 to the dollar, and Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  has repeatedly said the government has other tools to maintain the real at current levels.\par \par \uc2 News that \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services cut its rating on Spain and an interest rate cut by \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't enough to break the real out of its range.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively, and Mantega shoots his mouth off regularly," said Reginaldo Galhardo, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo. "It would take bigger news than (Spain and the rate cut) to move the real."\par \par \uc2 S&P cut its rating on Spain to BBB-minus from BBB-plus and maintained a negative outlook Wednesday, citing the mounting pressures from the country's deepening economic recession. S&P said the country's ability to repay its debt could deteriorate as it struggles to shrink its budget deficit, and that Spanish Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy might have to ask the European Union for a bailout.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided Wednesday night to reduce interest rates for the 10th straight time to spark local growth amid concerns about a prolonged global slump.\par \par \uc2 The bank cut its benchmark Selic interest rate a quarter-percentage point to a record low 7.25% and signaled the end to a cycle of cuts that started in August 2011.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting committee was divided, with five directors in favor of the cut and three directors voting to hold interest rates steady.\par \par \uc2 With the latest cut, the bank has reduced the benchmark Selic by 5.25 percentage points over the past 13 months.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian retail sales volume rose a seasonally adjusted 0.2% in August compared with July, the Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics, or IBGE, said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The increase was greater than the median forecast of analysts polled by the local Estado news agency, which projected sales volume to decrease 0.1% in August versus July. The forecasts fell within a range of a 0.9% decline and 1.0% growth.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeffrey T. Lewis at jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com [ 11-10-12 1336GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121011e8ab000vf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
