\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Trades Flat on the U.S. Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes   \par \uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2012\par 15:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real traded basically flat against the dollar Friday, recovering early losses after a key U.S. jobs report was released.\par \par \uc2 Around 9:20 a.m. EDT, the real was quoted in Sao Paulo trading BRL2.0169 to the dollar, virtually unchanged from its Thursday close of BRL2.0167.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had weakened to around BRL2.0190 in early trades, but recovered the loss after the U.S. Labor Department reported the unemployment rate fell to 7.8% in September from 8.1% in the previous month, the lowest level since January 2009. The U.S. economy added 114,000 jobs last month, according to the government's monthly survey of employers.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the European Union's official statistics agency said the euro zone's economy contracted faster than previously estimated in the second quarter in comparison to the same period last year.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics, or IBGE, said Friday that its official IPCA consumer price index rose at a quicker pace in September, as food-price pressures picked up during the month. The index gained 0.57% in September compared with a 0.41% rise in August.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast a 0.54% increase.\par \par \uc2 "In Brazil, the market is already eyeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary meeting, and today we saw that Brazil IPCA came above expectations," said Clodoir Vieira, economist at Brazil's brokerage \uc2 Souza Barros\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next monetary-policy meeting is scheduled for next week. Many economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its baseline Selic rate at the current 7.5%, a record low that followed a year-long easing cycle that brought rates down from 12.5% in August 2011.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and declarations from government officials aiming to protect local exporters have kept the real in a tight trading range between BRL2.00 and BRL2.05 in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 05-10-12 1347GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121005e8a5000sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
