\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes Essentially Steady Versus Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2012\par 22:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't react to uncertainties on Spain\par \par \uc2 --President Dilma Rousseff speaks in favor of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Real trades in a tight range in market of low liquidity\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Diana Kinch \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's real closed virtually stable against the dollar Tuesday, after the Brazilian government signaled a continued determination to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within a tight trading band, which reduces the impact of market moves world-wide.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited at BRL2.0246 to the dollar, marginally stronger than its Monday close of BRL2.0249, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real all day hovered midway in what is viewed as the government's chosen range, remaining impervious to new uncertainties over the Spanish economy that weighed on markets world-wide, economists said. The stability came after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's President Dilma Rousseff spoke in favor of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to aid Brazilian exporters' competitiveness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Rousseff admitted at an event in Sao Paulo late Monday that the government is continuing to adopt measures to keep the real from strengthening because this improves Brazil's competitiveness on international markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Brazilian real's increasingly set in a plaster cast," said Flavio Serrano, economist with Espirito Santo Investment Bank BES. "It shifted just a half-centavo all day."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government wants to avoid a significant weakening of the real, because that would be bad for inflation, and any significant strengthening, as that would be bad for industry and exporters, Mr. Serrano said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bruno Lavieri of Tendencias Consultoria said that Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime no longer can be considered floating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is stuck within the BRL2 to BRL2.10 band," Mr. Lavieri said. With its continued speeches on the possibility of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "the government's threatening not to let the real appreciate, and threatening to keep out the speculative capital from abroad."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "However, not only speculative capital, but also productive capital is being kept away by the new taxes, or threats of taxes," Mr. Lavieri said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is leading to reduced liquidity in the market, the economists said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real remained stable despite a "critical patch" in Europe, said Mr. Serrano.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets world-wide fell late Tuesday after Spain's prime minister, Mariano Rajoy, said the country won't imminently seek a new aid package from the European Union, despite its economic crisis, growing unemployment levels and movements toward regional separatism. The possibility that negotiations soon could start on a potential bailout package for Spain had buoyed markets earlier in the day. Such a package could allow the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy Spanish sovereign-debt papers to reduce its costs of financing Spain, according to market reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Spain really will need this help, but it's now uncertain when this will come and how much will be needed," Mr. Lavieri said. "Markets are in wait-and-see mode on Europe."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Diana Kinch at diana.kinch@dowjones.com [ 02-10-12 2049GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121002e8a2000vo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
