\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Ends Slightly Stronger as Market Monitors Dollar Flows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris  \par \uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2012\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRASILIA--Brazil's real climbed slightly to end stronger against the dollar Monday, as investors evaluated prospects for dollar inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The real exited regular trading at BRL2.0249 to the dollar after ending at BRL2.0263 to the dollar Friday, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved slightly stronger early in the session as investors reacted to signs inflation continued at accelerated levels. Brazil's Getulio Vargas Foundation early in the session reported its IPC-S consumer-price index rose 0.54% in September after a 0.44% advance in August.\par \par \uc2 Some doubts abroad helped sap the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  intraday, however, as worries over weak activity in China weighed against commodities exports from Brazil. China's Purchasing Managers Index, an important indicator of activity, rose 0.4 percentage point in September to 49.8, lower than market estimates of around 50.2.\par \par \uc2 The trend was offset later in the session by data in the U.S., where the Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing purchasing managers' index unexpectedly rose to 51.5 in September, beating estimates of a result around 49.5.\par \par \uc2 Analysts noted the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been showing only subdued reaction to such good news as traders look for strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility from the Brazilian government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The price of the dollar should continue being administered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  in an interval between BRL2.00 and BRL2.05," said Sidnei Nehme, trader at the NGO brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  against excessive variations of the real with recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap auctions.\par \par \uc2 Traders in the meantime are looking to see if a possible "flood" of incoming dollars from the U.S. Federal Reserve's third round of quantitative easing, announced last month, will work to strengthen Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the month of October.\par \par \uc2 However, if that doesn't materialize as expected, traders said a depreciation trend seen earlier this year could resume given recent lackluster investor interest in bringing money into Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 01, 2012 16:10 ET (20:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121001e8a100cd1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
