\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  firm on US factory data\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2012\par 19:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * US manufacturing sector expands for 1st time since May\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears curb \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso up 0.2 pct, Brazilian real nearly flat\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, changes dateline)\par \par \uc2 By Jean Arce\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Oct 1 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained on Monday after a surprise expansion in the U.S. manufacturing sector eased concerns about a global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso firmed 0.2 percent to 12.84 per U.S. dollar after data showed the manufacturing sector of the United States, Mexico's main trading partner, expanded in September for the first time since May.\par \par \uc2 "This scenario is positive for manufacturing activity, which obviously has an impact in the local market," said Mario Copca, an analyst at brokerage CI in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Mexican factories tend to closely track activity in the United States. An index of manufacturing activity in Mexico slowed for a third month in a row in September, but still pointed to further expansion ahead.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been revising their estimates for stronger Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as more dollars are expected to flow into emerging markets as a result of government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to stimulate growth.\par \par \uc2 Analysts now expect the peso to finish 2012 at 12.88 per dollar, stronger than the level of 13.01 forecast a month ago, a survey by the \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with private analysts showed.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  on Monday defended the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's controversial bond-buying stimulus plan.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers have complained that easy monetary policies in the United States are pushing speculators to buy up emerging market assets, strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and undermining the competitiveness of domestic manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real bid a slight 0.05 percent firmer at 2.0254 per dollar on the local market close. Investors have been avoiding making strong bets on a stronger real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers have managed to keep the real weaker than 2 per dollar -- a level they consider beneficial to exporters -- since early July.\par \par \uc2 "Here the concern is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step into the market if the dollar weakens too much. For now, nobody is willing to fight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mario Battistel, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at Fair Corretora, a brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the peso gained 0.4 percent, recovering part of the losses seen on Friday, when threats of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slump the most in seven weeks.\par \par \uc2 Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chile have grown after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Rodrigo Vergara said policymakers may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during "exceptional periods" in which the exchange rate is significantly out of line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The move by Chile pushed it closer to the stance of policymakers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Colombia and Peru who have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to fight dollar inflows.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2200 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0260      0.02     -7.77\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 12.8400      0.20      8.80\par                                              \par Argentina peso*              6.2400      0.96    -24.20\par                                              \par Chile peso                 472.6000      0.42      9.88\par                                              \par Colombia peso            1,800.1900      0.01      7.68\par                                              \par Peru sol                     2.5980     -0.08      3.81\par                                              \par * Argentine peso's rate between                        \par brokerages                                     \par                                                      \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli; editing by Andrew Hay)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121004e8a10013l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
