\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Opens Stronger Ahead of Spain Budget, Ptax Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2012\par 14:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil's real shrugs off concerns about European outlook weighing on euro\par \par \uc2 --Month-end battle to set real's reference rate likely to spark volatility\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real opens at BRL2.0294, stronger from Wednesday's close at BRL2.0320\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Jeff Fick \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--The Brazilian real traded stronger against the U.S. dollar at the open Thursday despite ongoing concerns about Europe's crisis and expected volatility as a month-end battle between local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders heated up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real shrugged off declines by the euro, a key barometer for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , ahead of the release of Spain's 2013 budget. Protests against austerity measures in Greece and Spain had rattled markets Wednesday. The real opened trading at BRL2.0294 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was stronger from Wednesday's close at BRL2.0320.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain is at the forefront of concerns in Europe, with the government set to approve its 2013 budget, which contains cutbacks aimed at easing the country's debt troubles. Austerity measures in Greece and Spain have sparked protests in each country and unnerved investors. The market will be watching closely for signs that Spain will request international financial aid, which it has so far resisted. Italy, meanwhile, carried out a successful bond sale, avoiding any spillover from the recent problems in its troubled euro-zone partners.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News that Spain was delaying the release of the 2013 budget until later Thursday briefly rattled local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, causing the real to weaken to BRL2.0321 to the dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  quickly recovered to again trade at BRL2.0294.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market also reacted to positive news out of China, where the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected nearly $58 billion into the financial system this week--including $8 billion overnight. While the move was directed at softening seasonal tightness in China's banking system, it could have a positive impact on Brazilian firms that export to the country. China is Brazil's largest trading partner.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Brazil, traders were also digesting the latest quarterly inflation report. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  boosted its outlook for 2012 inflation to 5.2% from the previous forecast of 4.7% on higher global commodity prices. Inflation, however, is expected to ease slightly next year as reductions on local energy rates take effect. In 2013, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expects inflation to end the year at 4.9% versus the previous forecast of 5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for Brazil's stumbling economy was also reduced. Latin America's largest economy has slowed dramatically since mid-2011, with the manufacturing sector stagnating under the weight of an overvalued real that made local goods less competitive and resulted in a flood of cheap imports. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expects Brazil to register gross domestic product growth of 1.6% in 2012, down from the previous forecast of 2.5% growth. In the second quarter, Brazil's economy expanded at an annual rate of 1.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders will closely watch developments abroad, the scene in Brazil will likely be dominated by the month-end battle to set the average daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate used to settle futures contracts, known as the Ptax. Long- and short-position holders typically engage in a tug-of-war to influence the reference rate, adding a measure of volatility to trading at the end of each month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite movements overseas, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent actions to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets mean the real is expected to remain locked into the current trading range between BRL2.00 and BRL2.05 to the dollar. The government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have made it clear to the market that they are comfortable with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at that level, with the real weak enough to support local manufacturers and exporters without affecting inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jeff Fick at Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 27-09-12 1232GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120927e89r000sf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
