\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slip on Spain protests\b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2012\par 19:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Madrid mass protests increase tensions in the euro zone\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real flat on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso 0.12 pct lower; Chilean peso down 0.11 pct\par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jean Arce\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged down on Wednesday as growing opposition to austerity measures in Spain added to jitters over the euro zone debt crisis, but recent stimulus measures in the United States were seen limiting losses.\par \par \uc2 Protests against austerity measures erupted in Madrid, making it more difficult for Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy to request international financial aid -- a condition for Spain to receive support from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors fear that a delay in the ECB bond-buying program would further complicate the euro-zone debt crisis, potentially dragging more economies into recession.\par \par \uc2 "The Spanish government is resisting the possibility of a asking for a bailout. It is still not clear what they will ask for and that is making the market nervous," said Juan Carlos Alderete, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banorte-IXE in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso lost 0.12 percent to 12.8670 per dollar while the Chilean peso bid 0.11 percent weaker at 470.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was near flat, however, in a seventh consecutive session of very low volatility. A recent bout of strong \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  left investors unwilling to make big bets on the direction of the real.\par \par \uc2 Despite expectations of short-term losses, traders said both the Mexican and Chilean pesos are set to gain in the medium term as U.S. stimulus measures increase dollar flows to emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The cost of dollars in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep rising, but it would likely hit resistance around 474 Chilean pesos per dollar, while losses in the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen limited to 13.05 or 13.10 per dollar, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The real was little changed as investors refrained from making bigger bets in a market that remains under constant threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Brazilian policymakers have bound the real to a very narrow trading range of 2.0 to 2.1 per dollar as they vow to use all means necessary to offset capital inflows resulting from the U.S. Federal Reserve's stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2 "Investors got scared with all this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk," said Gustavo Godoy, manager of the treasury desk of Daycoval bank in Sao Paulo. "You have the whole world to invest your money, why bring it to a market that is subject to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?"\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2045 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0337      0.03     -8.12\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 12.8760 