\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Calderon Critiques Fed But Sees No Need for  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2012\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Mexico's outgoing president warns against resumption of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Mr. Calderon, however, sympathizes with \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's complaints that loose U.S. monetary policy creates unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation\par \par \uc2 --He expresses doubt that QE works effectively\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Michael J. Casey \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outgoing Mexican President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Felipe Calderon\f28 \uc2 \f2  expressed sympathy Wednesday for other emerging-market leaders' complaints about how the U.S. Federal Reserve's loose monetary policy leads to the unwanted appreciation of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Mr. Calderon, whose six-year term ends when President-elect Enrique Pena Nieto assumes power on Dec. 1, also mounted a solid defense of his government's noninterventionist approach to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and recommended that his successor stick with it. He was speaking in an interview with editors and reporters from The Wall Street Journal and Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a thinly veiled criticism of the Fed's "quantitative-easing" bond-buying program, Mr. Calderon said serious discussion about its effectiveness was warranted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "How much of these efforts are going to boost local demand and how much is going to appreciate the real in Brazil?" he asked. "How much is going to the peso in Mexico?"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Calderon said the Fed's strategy, by which it purchases bonds with freshly printed dollars, might well be counterproductive because it drives up commodity prices, which squeezes household demand in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His comments followed Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff's speech to the U.N. General Assembly on Tuesday, where she said that "expansionary monetary policy" from developed-world central banks "aggravates the global recession." She said "emerging countries lose market share because of the artificial appreciation of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which aggravates the global recession."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the same remarks, Ms. Rousseff defended Brazil's response to this effect over the past two years, during which its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought dollars in the open market and it implemented taxes on fund inflows to limit their appreciative impact on the exchange rate. Since the Fed announced its third round of quantitative easing, or QE3, two weeks ago, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a number of occasions to weaken the real. Peru and other countries in Latin America have either done similarly or warned that they might do so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Mr. Calderon said he would never counsel his successor to abandon the hands-off approach Mexico has maintained during his tenure, even though the peso has risen more than other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of that policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I would not resume any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the markets," Mr. Calderon said, arguing instead that the country needed to instead boost its competitiveness by continuing to invest in infrastructure and education. Highlighting extensive programs that have added 20,000 kilometers of new highways over the past year, for example, and allowed Mexico to graduate 113,000 new engineers ever year, the outgoing president credited this approach with Mexico's rapid export growth and advances in value-added production.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although Mexico has done nothing to prevent loose U.S. monetary policy from driving up the peso and undermining competitiveness, Mexican exports now account for 60% of all Latin American exports, Mr. Calderon said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michael Casey\f28 \uc2 \f2  at michael.j.casey@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribe to WSJ: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 26-09-12 1731GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020120926e89q0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
