\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Current Account View Improves Amid Slow Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2012\par 23:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts projection for 2012 current account gap by $3 billion\par \par \uc2 --Overseas profit remittances slowing on tepid activity, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Investment inflows remain firm despite recent slowdown\par \par \uc2 By Gerald Jeffris\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil's current account deficit should be fully financed by incoming investment for a fifth consecutive year in 2012 as slow local and overseas activity weighs in against significant dollar outflows.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it would cut its projection for the current account deficit by $3 billion as reduced overseas remittances help rein in net dollar flows from the economy.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, however, local officials believe the economy will stand to benefit from growing inflows of foreign direct investment as the country moves toward a recovery.\par \par \uc2 "The country has consolidated itself as a net receiver of foreign direct investment," said Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economics Department Coordinator Tulio Maciel. "This reflects solid macroeconomic fundamentals and good prospects for growth."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that it raised its projection for FDI this year to $60 billion from $50 billion previously, in response to better-than-expected investment inflows seen in recent months. At the same time, it lowered its projection for the current account deficit to $53 billion from $56 billion.\par \par \uc2 Over 12 months through August, FDI in Brazil reached $65.8 billion, or the equivalent to 2.8% of gross domestic product. At the same time, the country's 12-month current account deficit in August came in at only to $49.7 billion, or 2.1% of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Maciel said the main driver behind a narrowing current account deficit should be lower overseas profit and dividend remittances by foreign companies with local \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We've seen moderate flows of profit and dividend remittances throughout the year," he said. "Economic activity has been slower, and the second factor is the stronger dollar--it's been more expensive to send remittances this year."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real has weakened by more than 10% against the dollar since earlier this year under the impact of risk aversion in international markets.\par \par \uc2 But while the current account remains fully covered by foreign investment for now, analysts still caution that the country shouldn't become overly confident the trend will continue.\par \par \uc2 "The capital account remains vulnerable to shifts in external sentiment and the unpredictable nature of the domestic policy mix," said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Alberto Goldman in a note Tuesday. Measures adopted by the government earlier this year to control capital inflows have "eroded sentiment toward \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials have actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local markets over recent months with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions in an effort to limit strengthening of Brazil's real, and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a narrow range between BRL2.00 and BRL2.10 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But despite the measures, investors for the present appear to remain interested in sending cash to the South American country. \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday reported net dollar inflows into the economy so far in September, bringing dollar inflows for the year to date to $23.66 billion.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 09-25-12 1746ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020120925e89p00093\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
