\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Weakens on President's Tough Line on  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2012\par 22:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real exits active trading at BRL2.0290/dlr versus BRL2.0245 Monday\par \par \uc2 --At U.N., President Rousseff says tough line against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation "legitimate"\par \par \uc2 --Continued worries about European debt crisis also weigh on Brazilian market\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real weakened against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as the country's president took a tough line against an unwanted appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading against the dollar at BRL2.0290, weaker than Monday's close of BRL2.0245, according to Tullet Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 In a speech at the United Nations, Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff said actions by her government to prevent the unwanted appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "legitimate." She told the General Assembly, "We cannot accept that legitimate initiatives of commercial defense by developing countries can be classified as protectionism... Protectionism and all the forms of commercial manipulation must be fought."\par \par \uc2 The president said central banks in industrially developed countries have taken "exaggerated steps" in combating a global slowdown, including a policy of flooding capital markets with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Much of that money, she said, finds its way to countries such as Brazil, where it leads to an unwanted appreciation of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Rousseff echoed the argument often made by Brazilian Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2 , who warned as recently as last week that any significant strengthening of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar would hurt exporters and manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 The message seems to be getting through to Brazilian investors.\par \par \uc2 "The government is virtually dictating the exchange rate these days," said Wanderlei Muniz, a trader at southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Onnix brokerage. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometimes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  even before there is any sign of volatility."\par \par \uc2 The constant possibility of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has effectively kept the Brazilian real weaker than BRL2.00 against the dollar since late May.\par \par \uc2 According to most analysts, the narrow band between BRL2.00 and BRL2.05 is likely to remain in place for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 "There is little reason to believe in any significant movement, one way or the other, for the Brazilian real in the near future," said Marcelo Carvalho, chief economist for Latin America at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  bank.\par \par \uc2 Frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have come mainly in the form of so-called reverse swap auctions at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up dollar-linked futures contracts. The effect is to reduce the availability of dollar-hedged paper in the market, thus making the dollar slightly more valuable against the real than before the auction.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has other tools it can use, including direct purchases of dollars from the market.\par \par \uc2 In comments to reporters last week, Finance Minister Mantega said the government was also prepared to use more powerful tools, such as higher taxes on short-term investment inflows. The government has used tax increases in the past to stem unwanted inflows.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Brazilian market on Tuesday was also jittery because of renewed worries about the European debt crisis, including reports that Greece is increasingly unable to meet austere budget goals and that Spain may soon be in need of a debt bailout.\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Tom Murphy\uc2  at tom.murphy@dowjones.com [ 25-09-12 2014GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120925e89p000x9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
