\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Opens Stronger as Exporters Sell Dollars\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2012\par 15:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real opens stronger at BRL2.0240 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 --Exporters sell dollars but trading volume is thin\par \par \uc2 --Little overseas capital entering Brazilian market\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real opened stronger against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as exporters sold dollars to the market.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.0240 to the dollar, stronger against the Monday close of BRL2.0245, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Traders said volume was thin.\par \par \uc2 "There is little or no investment capital entering the market," said Wanderlei Muniz, a trader at the Onnix brokerage in southern Brazil. "Investors know that any significant inflow of speculative capital will result in government action."\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in the market via auctions of so-called reverse swap contracts. The auctions tend to support the U.S. dollar against the Brazilian real by reducing the availability in the market of dollar-hedging contracts.\par \par \uc2 "The auctions are not the only tool the government has," said Muniz. "The government has ample tools to avoid market volatility."\par \par \uc2 In recent interviews, Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  raised the possibility of additional taxes on short-term investment inflows in the event of a significant appreciation of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. In recent years, Brazil's government has raised taxes on various types of investment in efforts to control what officials regard as "speculative" inflows.\par \par \uc2 Mantega and other officials have said any major appreciation of the real against the dollar would hurt exporters and manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff is expected to reinforce the message in a speech later Tuesday at the United Nations in New York. She is widely expected to criticize efforts by the U.S. Federal Reserve and other central banks to increase financial market liquidity as a way to stimulate economic growth. According to Brazilian officials, much of the enhanced liquidity flows to emerging market countries such as Brazil, leading to an unwanted appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With thin volume and cautious trading, traders foresee little volatility in the market Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "In fact, there is little prospect of volatility, going forward," said Marcelo Carvalho, chief economist for Latin America at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  bank in Sao Paulo. "The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have effectively set a narrow trading band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  anchored around the current value."\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Tom Murphy\uc2  at tom.murphy@dowjones.com [ 25-09-12 1303GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120925e89p000p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
