\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Chile peso dips on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  risk,  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real flat\b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2012\par 20:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real 'frozen' for 4th day on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2 * Chilean peso weakens after failing to break through 470 level\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real, Mexican peso trade flat\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Moises Avila and Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO/SAO PAULO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - Chile's peso weakened sharply on Friday on concerns that authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to st em c urrency gains that may hurt exporters while Brazil's real traded flat after a top official threatened to impl ement cap ital controls.\par \par \uc2 Investors are increasingly concerned that governments will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to offset capital inflows resulting from U.S. and European stimulus measures.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Colombia and Peru are already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, and some think Chile could be the next to act.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso lost 0.6 percent to 473.20 per dollar after it failed to break the psychological barrier of 470 per greenback.\par \par \uc2 "Traders are hesitant to add long positions below 470 on fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Katia Diaz, an economist with 4CAST in New York.\par \par \uc2 With gains of more than 10 percent so far in the year, the Chilean peso has \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that strengthened the most in 2012 an d it hit a one-year high this week.\par \par \uc2 Other traders said the peso slipped as investors took profits on the surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled a bond-buying program to support indebted countries and the Fed launched its own asset-purchase stimulus to boost growth in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly expected to voice its concern about an over-valued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, analysts said. A trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action could be a peso at 460 per dollar, they added.\par \par \uc2 "Close to 460, there will need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market. At that level the export sector will be less competitive and that will be reflected in the trade balance," said Carlos Rumie, trader at Celfin Capital in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real held steady for the fourth consecutive session, to bid at 2.0225 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  threatened to make use of short-term capital controls to curb any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that could hurt exporters.\par \par \uc2 "Selling the dollar and buying the real is a global trend. But local investors are not following that, they are mindful of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats from the Brazilian authorities," said Alfredo Barbutti, chief economist at BGC Liquidez brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week and on Monday to offset the appreciation trend. The bank has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then, but it is widely expected to act again if the real firms significantly.\par \par \uc2 So far, the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been able to keep the real weaker than 2 per dollar since early July with the sale of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps -- derivatives contracts that emulate the purchase of dollars in the futures market.\par \par \uc2 If dollar inflows pick up, however, it may resume outright dollar purchases in the spot market. If that fails, investors can probably count on the Finance Ministry to raise financial taxes on short-term capital flows.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso, one of the few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region that investors consider free of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, traded flat.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's could advance back toward a more than five-month high next week if Spain requests financial aid to help ease the country's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2100 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0218      0.01     -7.58\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 12.8700      0.00      8.54\par                                              \par Argentina peso*              6.3000      0.32    -24.92\par                                              \par Chile peso                 473.2000     -0.63      9.74\par                                              \par Colombia peso            1,797.3000     -0.20      7.85\par                                              \par Peru sol              