\par\par \b \uc2 Real Weakens Vs Dollar as  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Government Says Ready to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2012\par 22:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real weakens against the dollar after \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister speaks in London\par \par \uc2 --Real is set to stay within a narrow trading band\par \par \uc2 --Brazil government indicates it could seek further interest-rate cut\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Diana Kinch \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's real weakened against the dollar in late trading Friday, after Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guido Mantega\f28 \uc2 \f2  made it plain the government is ready for market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciates beyond a desired level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.0225, marginally weaker than its Thursday close of BRL2.0211, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists said comments made by Mr. Mantega in London neutralized the impact on the real of rising risk appetite in markets world-wide after The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Times\f28 \uc2 \f2  published news of a debt-alleviation package for Spain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had lost ground against the euro and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  early Friday after the FT revealed that Spain's government is in negotiations with European Union authorities on a possible new rescue package to be announced Sept. 27. This would involve the purchase of Spanish bonds by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the implementation of structural reforms to meet the demands of international creditors, the newspaper said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Mr. Mantega's explicit declarations at The Economist's High-Growth Markets Summit kept the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stable and even reduced trading volumes as traders remained aware of the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be via reverse swap auctions, equivalent to the purchase of dollars on the futures market, or by other measures that could include taxes on the entry of funds into Brazil, the minister said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Mantega reinforced what he had already said. They're putting a price tag on the real, at between BRL2 to BRL2.05," said Jankiel Santos, economist with Espirito Santo Investment Bank, or BES.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This trading range already has been virtually enforced by the government for the past two to three months, according to Silvio Campos Neto, economist with Tendencias Consultoria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government's controlling (the market), they're getting more explicit," Mr. Campos Neto said. "The tone (of the statements) is stronger. They're not going to allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to free float."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the event in London, Mr. Mantega slammed the U.S. Federal Reserve's recent decision to launch a third-round of quantitative easing, saying it stimulates \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars and stressed that whatever action is necessary to prevent speculative flows from flooding into his country as a result of a weaker dollar will be taken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Quantitative easing, in my view, does not have very much effect in terms of recovering the economy because it is done in isolation and not done in combination with fiscal policy," said Mr. Mantega at the summit, adding that all it does in the end is devalue the dollar, which in turn stimulates \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Mantega added that Brazil's government will continue to use monetary policy to stimulate growth. He said there is still space for a further reduction of the Selic base interest rate, which currently stands at an all-time low of 7.5%. Economists in Brazil criticized the minister's comments, which indicate that the government is seeking to apply pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is supposed to act as an autonomous and independent body.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's clear there's great government pressure to cut the interest rates further," Mr. Campos Neto said. "The government wants a further 0.25% interest rate cut."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary-policy council is due to meet Oct. 10 to decide on its forthcoming interest-rate policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Clare Connaghan contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Diana Kinch at diana.kinch@dowjones.com [ 21-09-12 2042GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120921e89l000vy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
