\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso slips on Spain worries, Brazil real up\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2012\par 19:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Investors push Spain to seek financial aid\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Effect of Fed stimulus wanes\par \par \uc2 By Natalia Cacioli and Louise Egan\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso weakened o n T uesday as concerns about Spain's willingness to ask for an aid package curbed investor appetite for emerging market assets, offsetting any strengthening effect from the latest round of U.S. monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real ended slightly stronger, however, and even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it did on Monday, Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  made it clear policymakers would not hesitate to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating. [ID: nL5E8KICU6]\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso, Latin America's most traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slipped 0.22 percent to 12.7950 per dollar as investors worried that the European debt crisis could get worse again if Spain delays a request for international aid they deem as inevitable.\par \par \uc2 It was the second straight day of minor setbacks for the peso after it had surged to more than a five-month high on Friday on bets that the U.S. Federal Reserve's third round of bond-buying would push a flood of cash into the region.\par \par \uc2 But the effects of the Fed's aggressive move so far seem milder than in the previous two rounds of money printing, said Enrique Alvarez, senior economist at IDEAglobal, though it is still too early to say for certain.\par \par \uc2 "The initial impact at this point is being attenuated by the usual jitters on the side of Europe," he said.\par \par \uc2 "As long as that occurs, it takes away from the positives that were set in the market by the Fed's latest plan to go ahead and buy mortgages."\par \par \uc2 The lack of volume and of key economic data left the market more vulnerable to swings in investor sentiment, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the real bid 0.35 percent stronger at 2.0225 per dollar at the local market close as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the first session in three. Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained within the narrow range of 2.0-2.1 per greenback it has been trading in since early July.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  left the real decoupled from overseas markets," said Jankiel Santos, chief economist at BES Investimentos in Sao Paulo. "As much as investors want to sell dollars, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  much more than expected and that has kept the real at weaker levels."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to offset possible dollar inflows stemming from the stimulus measures announced by the U.S. Federal Reserve last week.\par \par \uc2 In less than one week, the Brazilian bank has sold about $5.7 billion worth of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps in four separate auctions, whenever the real strengthened toward the level of 2 per dollar. Those swaps emulate the purchase of dollars in the futures market, weakening the real as a result.\par \par \uc2 In a sign that the government could make use of yet other measures to keep a lid on the real, Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  on Tu esday repeated that Brazil will continue to take measures to offset the impact of the Fed's monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 In Peru, the sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished stable after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $40 million on the local spot market.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, financial markets were closed for a national holiday and will reopen on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2130 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0226      0.35     -7.62\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 12.8000     -0.25      9.14\par                                              \par Argentina peso*              6.2900      0.16    -24.80\par                                              \par Chile peso                 470.6000       0.0     10.35\par                                              \par Colombia peso            1,795.5000      0.17      7.96\par                                              \par Peru sol                     2.6020      0.00      3.65\par                                              \par * Argentine peso's rate between                        \par brokerages                                     \par                                                      \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Lizbeth Salazar and Louise Egan in Mexico City; Editing by Dan Grebler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LAT