\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Finance Minister: Europe Policies are Always Behind Events\b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2012\par 14:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:51 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Developed World will See Low Growth For 'Various' Years\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:51 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega Speaks at Event in Sao Paulo\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:55 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Global Outlook for 2013 'Not Very Favorable'\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:55 EDT *DJ  Mantega: Brazil Recovery is Taking Longer Than Expected\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:55 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega Reiterates 4% GDP Growth Forecast for 2013\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:58 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Nominal Deficit Should Br 1% of GDP in 2013\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 08:59 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Inflation is 'Under Control', Despite Grain Price Shock\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 09:00 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Tax Cuts to Have 'Positive' Effect on Inflation Next Year\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 09:08 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Bond Market to Benefit from Lower Selic Rate\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 09:10 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Exchange Rate Policy Doesn't Allow Appreciation of Real\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 09:10 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Will Maintain 'Active' \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2012 09:48 EDT DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Outlook for Global Growth Isn't Favorable\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The outlook for global growth isn't very favorable, and developed world countries are likely to experience low growth for several years, Brazil's finance minister, Guido Mantega, said at an event on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The impression I have is that advanced countries won't get out of this crisis very soon. They will continue with low growth for several years," Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 Europe's policies are "always behind in relation to the problems," he said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's recovery is taking longer than expected, with only a gradual acceleration of growth starting in the third quarter, the minister said. He reiterated his forecast for 4% gross domestic product growth in 2013, and said that would be the pace in years ahead as well.\par \par \uc2 The minister said that Brazil's consumer demand will continue to grow in the next few years. The country has full employment, and is expected to create 1.6 million formal jobs this year, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Those that said the model of expanding the consumer market is exhausted are wrong," he said.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, "despite what many say, investment in Brazil has grown more than consumption," Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue with its current policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We have a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy which doesn't allow the appreciation of the real," Mr. Mantega said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  today at around BRL2.00 per dollar "improves the competitiveness of Brazilian companies," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real is supposed to be a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the government has in effect introduced a "dirty float" with some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and targeted capital controls to prevent what it considers to have been an over-appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years. The real had strengthened as far as BRL1.55 per dollar in mid-2011.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue with an active \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy until all the economies actually adopt a floating exchange rate," Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 The official said that, even with the tax cuts and spending increases to help growth, the government will reduce its nominal deficit next year, to 1% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is "under control," despite the recent increase in global grains prices, he said. Recent tax cuts haven't yet taken effect, and will have a "positive" effect on inflation next year.\par \par \uc2 The country is "close to self-sufficiency" in oil, even before the country has begun to exploit massive fields discovered in recent years off the country's south-east coast.\par \par \uc2 The bond market was prevented from growing because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key overnight rate, the Selic, was very high, Mr. Mantega said. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed the rate to a record low of 7.5% -- from 12.5% a year ago -- real interest rates in Brazil have fallen below 2%.\par \par \uc2 Government borrowing becomes less significant at these levels and companies will now be better able to finance themselves through the use of debentures, he said.\par \par \uc2 By 2020, Brazil will be the largest exporter of food in the world, according to the U.N.'s Food and Agriculture Organization, Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Rogerio Jelmayer at rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Matthew Cowley in Sao Paulo contributed\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 14, 2012 09:48 ET (13:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120914e89e0034c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
