\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Brazil tackles weak economy with flurry of stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2012\par 21:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Sept 13 (Reuters) - Brazil has rolled out more than a dozen stimulus measures since a sharp slowdown in late 2011, trying to bolster a sluggish recovery in the world's sixth-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 In the latest move, President Dilma Rousseff's government extended targeted tax breaks on Thursday to reduce payroll taxes for 25 industries including pulp producers, meatpackers and freight transportation.\par \par \uc2 The following are other government initiatives aimed at rekindling domestic demand and reviving struggling industries:\par \par \uc2 LOWER INTEREST RATES\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed borrowing costs by 5 percentage points in 12 months, bringing its benchmark interest rate to a record low 7.5 percent in August.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate cuts and direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market have helped to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real. A weaker real helps make Brazilian goods more competitive on global markets and gives local producers an edge against imports.\par \par \uc2 TAX BREAKS FOR MANUFACTURED GOODS\par \par \uc2 The government has also offered temporary tax relief to spur consumption of manufactured goods including: cars, home appliances and building materials.\par \par \uc2 STRONG-ARMING BANKS\par \par \uc2 Government-controlled banks and political speeches have pressed private-sector lenders to reduce interest rates charged to consumers, passing on record low borrowing costs to encourage household consumption.\par \par \uc2 PROTECTIONISM\par \par \uc2 Brazil plans to raise tariffs on 100 foreign products to protect local manufacturers from a flood of imports. The increased barriers follow protections of the domestic auto industry, including a steep tax hike on foreign-made cars and a quota on Mexican auto imports.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT PURCHASES\par \par \uc2 The government is expanding its purchases of military, educational and medical equipment, giving preference to Brazilian-made products.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIZED CREDIT FOR INVESTMENT\par \par \uc2 State development bank BNDES cut rates on its subsidized loans and offered new credit lines for machinery and other capital goods. Cheap loans to states and incentives for public-private partnerships were also aimed at lifting Brazil's slipping investment rate.\par \par \uc2 PRIVATIZING AIRPORTS, HIGHWAYS AND RAILROADS\par \par \uc2 Rousseff, whose leftist Workers' Party once railed against privatizations, has opened major airports, highways and railroads to private investment in order to reduce transport costs and improve industrial competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 LOWER ENERGY BILLS\par \par \uc2 The government plans to cut electricity taxes and renegotiate utility concessions in a bid to lower energy costs for industries and households, which are currently among the world's highest.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Brad Haynes; Editing by Todd Benson and James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/MEASURES (FACTBOX) | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | O | OIL | U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120916e89d0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
