\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil stocks a buy as risk aversion fades-Santander\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2012\par 21:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Domestic-oriented stocks expected to take lead\par \par \uc2 * Investors may be making wrong assumptions about growth\par \par \uc2 * Foreign investors seen returning as recovery picks up\par \par \uc2 * Bovespa index could climb to 70,000 by year-end\par \par \uc2 By Guillermo Parra-Bernal and Asher Levine\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 11 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks are a buy because growth in Latin America's largest economy is recovering from a yearlong slowdown and the effect of excessive risk aversion on its economy is easing, strategists at Santander Global Banking & Markets said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 With an industry slowdown on the verge of turning and credit markets recovering as interest rates fall to record lows, shares in financial, real estate, transportation, retail and capital goods companies, known as domestic cyclicals, should gain, said Marcelo Audi, Santander GB&M's equity strategist for Brazil.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Bovespa stock index, which is now trading at about 59,200 points, could reach levels somewhere between 65,000 and 70,000 by year-end as foreign investors ease concerns over growth and return to the region's biggest equity market, Audi said.\par \par \uc2 "Risk perception among investors for a while leaned towards pessimism, for the wrong reasons," Audi said at a presentation at the bank's headquarters in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo. "Now we say: it's time to buy Brazilian stocks."\par \par \uc2 Audi's call comes as Santander prepares to host more than 180 key clients at a summit in the Brazilian beach resort of Guaruj\u225\'00\'E1 this week - in which the bank will advise them to add exposure to Brazil's equity markets after two disappointing years for the Bovespa.\par \par \uc2 The Bovespa, which is up about 4 percent this year, was almost flat in 2010 before tumbling 18 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 Audi and his team are telling investors to raise allocations in Brazilian domestic cyclicals by a double-digit pace for the first time since mid-2009. Companies in that group include clearinghouse Cetip, capital goods maker Randon Participa\u231\'00\'E7\u245\'00\'F5es, clothing maker Hering and \uc2 Grupo P\u227\'00\'E3o de A\u231\'00\'E7\u250\'00\'FAcar\uc2 , the nation's largest retailer.\par \par \uc2 Analysts disagree over the outlook for corporate earnings in the third and fourth quarters following a slate of disappointing results in the second quarter of the year. Many companies reported weak earnings because of slowing domestic demand and the effects of a declining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on existing debt, though Audi said that stronger readings of economic data are likely to more than offset the final stage of the "downgrading phase" in earnings estimates.\par \par \uc2 Domestic cyclicals will gain more weight on the Bovespa index in the coming years, chiefly at the expense of commodity-oriented shares - which right now account for nearly half the index.\par \par \uc2 Audi dismissed the idea that Brazil is losing its appeal among foreign investors as it becomes more difficult and costlier to do business in the country. Investors will return to the local equity market lured by the nation's growth potential.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors flush with cheap capital provided by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  early this year helped drive the Bovespa to a nearly 14 percent gain in the first quarter. However, deepening worries over the euro zone debt crisis and concern over heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy caused those gains to reverse in the second quarter. Since June, foreign investors have cautiously returned to Brazilian stocks in search of inexpensive shares and high returns.\par \par \uc2 Santander's chief economist, Mauricio Molan, expects gross domestic product to expand above 1.5 percent in the third quarter on a sequential basis, later speeding to a 4.2 percent growth rate next year, though below 4 percent for the next decade.\par \par \uc2 While Molan sees inflation under control at 5.4 percent this year, he expects recent stimulus measures enacted by Brazil's government to contribute to a 6 percent inflation rate in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Santander GB&M is the investment-banking and securities unit of \uc2 Banco Santander Brasil\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Guillermo Parra-Bernal and Asher Levine; Editing by M.D. Golan)\par \par \uc2 SANTANDERBRASIL-BRAZIL/EQUITIES | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | U | ABX | BNX | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120914e89b0018u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
