\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Stronger Early Following Industrial Production\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2012\par 14:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--Brazil's real strengthened slightly in early trading Tuesday amid signs from industrial production data that the country's economy may be warming.\par \par \uc2 As of 1240 GMT, the real traded at BRL2.0282 to the dollar after ending at BRL2.0302 to the dollar Monday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the real inched stronger at the open after the country's IBGE statistics institute reported that industrial output rose by 0.3% in July from the previous month. Though the figure was lower than July last year, it beat analyst forecasts of a 0.2% gain.\par \par \uc2 The latest data helped reinforce expectations that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ongoing rate easing cycle could soon be brought to an end, pointing to stable or possible higher interest rates down the road.\par \par \uc2 The figures also brought some hopes that flows of investment into the country would resume after a slowdown of the economy seen in the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Also boosting the real Tuesday were rising commodities prices abroad amid expectations key central banks abroad could introduce measures to spur increased growth. In particular, investors are looking to actions by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a policy meeting Thursday to ease monetary policy or increase purchase of sovereign debt from troubled countries in the region. The wait also continues on the other side of the Atlantic, amid continued speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve could soon announce another round of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 Investors also continued to be on the lookout for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a competitive rate.\par \par \uc2 The bank this week allowed some $4 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to expire in an effort to hold the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at recent levels.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  last week, meanwhile, guaranteed that the government would maintain an "active" stance in the market in reaction to signs that the real might strengthen too far and compromise the competitiveness of local industries.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 04-09-12 1245GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120904e894000q2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
