\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso up on Fed minutes, Brazil real down\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2012\par 22:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Investors eye \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings next week\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real down 0.07 pct, Mexico's peso gains 0.52\par \par \uc2 By Anna Irrera\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Aug 22 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso gained on Wednesday after minutes of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's last policy meeting bolstered bets for another round of monetary stimulus, while Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost slightly.\par \par \uc2 Prompted by concerns about slow economic growth in the world's largest economy, the Fed's meeting minutes from August suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to provide another round of stimulus "fairly soon" unless conditions improve.\par \par \uc2 Monetary stimulus in the United States tends to drive down yields on Treasuries, boosting the appeal of higher-yielding emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 Still, some analysts were not completely confident the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would deliver further monetary policy stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Since the meeting, there's been a spate of upbeat U.S. economic news that could counter the bank's willingness to open up a third round of bond-buying, known as quantitative easing or QE3.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the minutes are decisive indication that the Fed will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  in New York. "I'm not sure that this alone will provide sustained positive momentum (of the Mexican peso). Markets are still very much focused on whether European tensions are going to ease in the coming weeks."\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso strengthened 0.52 percent to 13.0888 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real bid down -0.07 to 2.0186 per dollar at the local market close. The real traded in a narrow range as investors remained concerned that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had not waded into the market for several weeks, on Tuesday sold $350.2 million of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts, which mimic the purchase of U.S. dollars in futures markets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen again signaling to markets that it wanted to keep the real from reaching a point where it would hurt local industries competing against cheaper imports.\par \par \uc2 The real has been mostly trading between 2 per dollar and 2.05 per dollar since the end of June on the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition to acting to protect local exporters, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policymakers have acted to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it weakens so much that it could raise import prices and stoke inflation.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been worried about slowing global growth as the euro zone crisis drags on.\par \par \uc2 To ease the crisis and spur growth, market players are looking for stimulus relief in the form of bond-buying programs. Both the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and ECB hold policy meetings next month that could decide which, if any, monetary measures they will take to address these problems.\par \par \uc2 GROWTH EXPECTATIONS IN CHILE\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the peso advanced 0.19 percent to 481.7000 further boosted by the expectation the economy will grow strongly and interest rates will remain steady.\par \par \uc2 That would boost revenue for companies while maintaining returns for investors. Interest rates in the United States, Europe and Japan are at or near zero and rates are falling in much of Latin America.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll of traders said the bank is likely to hold its benchmark rate at 5.0 percent for three months.\par \par \uc2 A the same time, Chile's economy is expanding at a rate of about 5.0 percent, a trend that is likely to continue for the near term if economic conditions remain stable, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said on Tuesday. The estimate is part of planning for the country's 2013 budget.\par \par \uc2 "The country is passing through a good moment despite the difficulties abroad, and in our region," he said.\par \par \uc2 Larrain added that the government raised its long-term price estimate for copper to 3.06 a pound from 3.02 a pound. Copper is responsible for more than half the country's exports.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1957 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                Daily  Year-to-\par                                          pct   ate pct\par                             Latest    change    change\par Brazil real                   2.0186     -0.15     -7.44\par                                               \par Mexico peso                  13.0888      0.52      6.73\par                                               \par Argentina peso*               6.3700     -0.31    -25.75\par                                               \par Chile peso                  481.7000      0.19      7.81\par                                               \par Peru sol                      2.6140     -0.08      3.18\par                                               \par * Argentine peso's rate between                         \par brokerages         