\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Vale Nickel Risks Rise, Investors Wary -\uc2 Barclays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2012\par 19:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:13 EDT - Brazilian miner Vale's (VALE VALE5.BR) VNC nickel project in New Caledonia, where minority partner \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  will reportedly register losses, is set to bring further risks for VALE, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. Project has been very problematic so far, with technical problems during ramp-up, higher-than-expected costs and a new technology -- high pressure acid leaching -- which is still unproven in the industry, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. "Vale's base metals division has been a source of concern to investors given its capital requirements, low returns and potential impairment of assets at VNC and On\u231\'00\'E7a Puma," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. The "headline risk should increase going forward." In NY, VALE up 0.5% at $18.06. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EDT - \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  (BBY) shares have recovered from their early depths as investors apparently look beyond F2Q's ugly headlines, even pushing the stock briefly into positive territory. Despite a bottom-line plunge and suspended guidance, executives don't expect last quarter's weakness to persist into 2H to the same degree. Ignoring prior guidance is partly on expected volatility from coming product launches, but Janney notes new products will likely boost \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so instead it blames macro and profit-cycle pressures for the latest weakness, not structural splintering that would justify a fundamental selloff. Still, yesterday's 10% dive and the current 1.6% drop to $17.86 puts BBY shares where they were when \uc2 Richard Schulze\uc2 's proposed buyout went public. (joan.solsman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:53 EDT - Chuck Butler, president of EverBank Global Markets, says he had a neutral position in the euro heading into Tuesday's euro rally and intends to stay out of the euro until mid-September, after the German constitutional court has delivered a verdict on the constitutionality of the euro-zone bailout fund. He says the euro rally may not last much longer, but EUR/USD shorts could also "get knocked out if we see a brief move above 1.25." Butler recommends buying the Chinese yuan in the meantime because the depreciating yuan presents a cheap entry, adding that he expects it to appreciate over time. Euro last trades at $1.2471, up 1% from $1.2346 late Monday. (nicole.hong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:52 EDT - Just as market participants were waiting for the dollar to edge closer to BRL2, in sync with a rally in all higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announces reverse swap auctions to buy dollars. That indicates the bank's comfortable range for the pair is 2-2.10, says Citi. The move is the first dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 26. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a bid to boost economic growth, has been weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though some analysts have feared doing so would lead to higher inflation. The dollar is currently at BRL2.0158, slightly down from Monday's close of BRL2.0173, according to CQG. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:43 EDT - In part worried about competition from Christie's over market share, fellow auctioneer \uc2 Sotheby's\uc2  (BID) gets downgraded to underperform at Williams Capital, which cuts 2012 estimates and lowers its price target to $23 from $28. "We expect \uc2 Sotheby's\uc2  to experience a greater sales and earnings decline in 2012 than we were originally expecting with declining auction margins." That as the investment bank is "increasingly concerned that the global art market may not be able to match the strength of last year." It notes BID saw "notable" 1H sales declines amid fewer auctions and "we have no reason to believe that the marketplace is going to improve in the near-term." BID is down 1.4% at $31.85, but it's up 8.5% for the month. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:36 EDT - Natural gas traders, glued to weather forecasts as they try to gauge demand for the fuel, should also keep an eye on the coal market. Gas and coal compete directly for US power-generation market share. Gas won the battle for much of the year. It was cheap, and the prospect that tough new federal air pollution rules would survive legal challenges made utilities reluctant to buy their typical amount of coal. But after a federal court ruled the EPA must revise a key regulation, utilities may rethink their coal buying plans. Nymex gas futures hit intraday lows minutes after the court ruling, but recover to trade up 0.8% at $2.80/MMBtu. (matt.day@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - For retail traders who need to get approval from their brokerage to trade more complex option strategies, the Chicago Board Options Exchange is offering a solution. CBOE announced it will launch S&P 500 Range options (SRO) next Tuesday to provide a "lower-risk, lower-cost way to trade S&P 500 index options," the exchange operator said. The options allow traders to target a range for expiration with different payout odds if it falls in that range. The max loss becomes the price paid for the options. "We are looking to bring new users into the options market with limited risk and limited reward," said William Speth, a VP of research at CBOE. (kaitlyn.kiernan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - The Canadian dollar has seen some strong recent gains, but the next move for the US dollar could be higher -- particularly if the Fed waits until after the US election to adopt further stimulus. (And it might, given a recent string of better US data.) For the loonie, reasons to rally further "are in short supply," with the odds of a \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate hike unlikely to build amid the recent weaker Canadian data. Risks from Europe and China, meanwhile, are USD-positive, commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  negative. "Should any of those risks materialize, the [Canada dollar] would easily slip back a few cents weaker than parity, consistent with our target of 1.02 USD/CAD by year-end," CIBC says. US dollar recently at C$0.9866, from C$0.9884 late Monday. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:23 EDT - The Dow industrials' move into negative territory for the day comes as the index just failed to reach a fresh multiyear high. It got to 13330.76 today, versus the May 2 peak of 13338.66. Getting above that would have put the DJIA at its highest level seen since Dec. 28, 2007. Meanwhile, the S&P 500's intraday high of 1426.69 easily surpassed its 2012 top of 1422.38 to reach its highest point since May 19, 2008. The DJIA is down 3 at 13268 and the S&P is up 1.5 at 1419.63. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Conde Nast introduced digital editions of "Architectural Digest" and "Lucky" magazines on \uc2 Adobe\uc2 's (ADBE) Digital Publishing Suite this week. Add to that the March debut of digital "Vogue" and it means 14 of the publisher's 18 magazines now have editions tailored for tablets and smart phones powered by DPS. The publisher is pursuing a "steady digital roll out," a spokeswoman said, but wouldn't disclose what title would be next. ADBE says publishers are using DPS to deliver digital editions of about 3,000 titles. ADBE is about flat at $33.94. (steve-d.jones@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:17 EDT - A spate of oilfield-services deals shows the M&A market is placing a higher value on targets than stock investors, says Tudor Pickering. Investor reaction has been "subdued" to deals that should boost the earnings of buyers, including National Oilwell (NOV), \uc2 FMC Technologies\uc2  (FTI) and \uc2 Chicago Bridge & Iron\uc2  (CBI) while most of the targets have "healthy" exposure to North America. In the case of CBI target SHAW, it's in the "out-of-favor power market." Tudor Pickering notes both power and North America are areas that "investors still view skeptically." (ryan.dezember@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:15 EDT - The New York Fed said it completed sales of 65 residential mortgage bonds from its Maiden Lane III portfolio of assets taken on during AIG's bailout in 2008. The bonds, with total face value of $3.3M, represent the second-to-last sale from the portfolio, with about $3B of CDOs set for auction Thursday. (albert.yoon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-21-12 1313ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120821e88l000d1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
