\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Textiles Would Benefit From Weaker Real -Coteminas CEO\b0\par\par\uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2012\par 23:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real not weak enough to boost textile exports\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  imbalances should be discussed at the WTO\par \par \uc2 --Lower interest rates helping stabilize Brazil's exchange rate\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Luciana Magalhaes \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO--Exchange-rate misalignments are still at the heart of the competitiveness problem faced by Brazil's industrial sector, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josue Gomes da Silva\f28 \uc2 \f2 , chief executive officer of textile giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coteminas\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market and the systematic reduction of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's base interest rate have helped to weaken the real in relation to the dollar, the textile industry could benefit from an even weaker real, the executive said in an interview.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. da Silva said the current exchange rate of about BRL2.00 to the dollar eliminates the threat of heavy textile imports into Brazil that existed earlier this year, when the real traded around BRL1.70 against the greenback. "But I don't believe it creates the possibility of a significant rate of textile exports by the Brazilian industry," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coteminas\f28 \uc2 \f2  is directly affected by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trends. The company was able to multiply its exports more than six-fold from 2001 through 2007 because of a comparatively weak real. But a stronger Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the last five years has erased those gains. "Our exports in 2011 were similar to those of 2001," said Mr. da Silva.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The company, Brazil's largest textile conglomerate, was founded by Mr. Da Silva's father, former Brazilian Vice President Jose Alencar Gomes da Silva.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Da Silva said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coteminas\f28 \uc2 \f2  was increasingly focused on the domestic market. He added that even if the exchange rate were to turn more favorable, he would still be cautious about boosting exports. "Exchange-rate trends can change," he noted. "And the countries we compete with have undervalued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. da Silva declined to speculate on what would be an "equilibrium rate" for the Brazilian real today, saying that was up to economists, who possess "the right methodologies to calculate such misalignments."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, he warned that a comparatively strong real was generally undermining Brazilian industry in a process economists have dubbed "de-industrialization."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the executive said, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  asymmetries aren't a problem solely for Brazil. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  imbalances should be part of a global trade discussion to be addressed at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Trade Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is so crucial that it needs to be discussed by the WTO, and I think they are recognizing that," Mr. da Silva said. He participated earlier this year in debates promoted by the WTO to discuss the relationship between exchange rates and international trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the specific case of Brazil, Mr. da Silva said an important change towards a more neutral exchange rate has been the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's drive to reduce the country's sky-high interest rates. Brazil's Selic base rate has been cut from the 2011 peak of 12.5% to 8%. "Maybe a good by-product of the international crisis, if there is one, is that Brazil was able to break a long-lasting policy of high interest rates," he said. "As [former Brazilian Finance Minister] Mario Henrique Simonsen used to say, 'interest rates handicap, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kills.'"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribe to WSJ: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\uc2 \f2  [ 08-15-12 1703ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120815e88f0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
