\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso up on rising inflation, Brazil real gains\b0\par\par\uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2012\par 23:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazilian real seen testing 2-per-dollar level\par \par \uc2 * Colombia finmin "prays" for stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real gains 0.28 pct, Mexico peso up 0.53 pct\par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers; Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Jean Arce\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso gained on T hursday as high inflation boded steady interest rates ahead while Brazil's real got a lift after weak factory data in China backed hopes of more stimulus in Brazil's top trading partner.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's annual inflation accelerated to its highest rate in more than two years in July on a surge in egg and fresh produce prices, reinforcing bets that policymakers will hold interest rates steady at 4.50 percent in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Mexico has held interest rates steady this year even as Brazil slashed borrowing costs to support growth. Rock-bottom interest rates in the United States and Europe have boosted the appeal of higher-yielding Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexican annual inflation rose to 4.42 percent last month, above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4 percent comfort zone. Even though there are signs Mexican growth may be slowing, high inflation should keep policymakers from cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Lower benchmark rates would curb the gains investors can make from borrowing in dollars or euros to invest in Mexican fixed-income assets.\par \par \uc2 "With expectations that inflation could stay above 4 percent in the coming months, we think that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico cannot cut interest rates," said Rafael Camarena, an economist at Santander in Mexico City. "In the short term, this helps the peso."\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso strengthened 0.53 percent to 13.0860 per dollar, also boosted by data showing claims for unemployment benefits unexpectedly fell in the United States, Mexico's main trading partner.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, expectations for additional Chinese stimulus rose after data showed growth in the country's factory output slowed to the weakest pace in three years.\par \par \uc2 China is the largest consumer of Latin American commodities, and prices of raw materials have great sway over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real gained 0.28 percent to 2.0160 per dollar, leading some analysts to forecast the market could soon test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to defend the level of 2 reais per dollar, considered by investors an informal floor for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The real is nearing the level of 2 per dollar and, with a possible decline in risk aversion, markets may test this level today," said Andre Perfeito, chief economist at Gradual Investimentos in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Government officials have repeatedly said that a real stronger than 2 per dollar could hurt Brazilian exporters, leading investors to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step into the market to defend that level.\par \par \uc2 The tide of yield-hungry global investors has been strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around Latin America and pressuring exporters.\par \par \uc2 Colombian Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry called on industrialists to "pray" that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts dollar purchases to ease gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after noting foreign direct investment in Colombia could reach $17 billion this year and boost the peso.\par \par \uc2 Echeverry said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is independent from the government, should step up dollar purchases to $40 million a day to strengthen its arsenal to fight the gaining peso. The bank currently buys at least $20 million a day on the spot market.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2100 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             daily %  year-to-\par                                      change     ate %\par                            Latest              change\par Brazil real                  2.0145      0.36     -7.25\par                                              \par Mexico peso                 13.0860      0.53      6.75\par                                              \par Argentina peso*              6.2300      0.48    -24.08\par                                              \par Chile peso                 474.8000      0.29      9.37\par                                              \par Colombia peso               1788.00     -0.01      8.65\par                                              \par Peru sol                     2.6160      0.00      3.10\par                                              \par * Argentine peso's rate between                        \par brokerages                                     \par                                                      \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota; Editing by Dan Grebler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)(REPEAT) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120812e889000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\pics